export default "# AH distilled: compact knowledge base\n\n## BOOK: $100M Offers (How to make offers so good people feel stupid saying no)\nLink: offers/\n\n### Grand Slam Offer (link: offers/grand-slam-offer.html)\nTerm: Grand Slam Offer\nCard: An offer so valuable for its price, and so specific to one market, that the buyer has nothing to compare it with. It is the way out of competing on price.\nKey rule: Commodity → Differentiated → Grand Slam (category of one)\nGist: A **commodity** is anything the buyer can compare line by line with what someone else sells. Same hours, same features, same deliverables. When the buyer can compare everything, the only thing left to decide on is price, and the cheapest seller wins a race nobody profits from.\n\nA **Grand Slam Offer** is built so that comparison stops working. It solves every problem one specific market has on the way to its dream outcome, it is worth far more than it costs, and it is packaged in a way nobody else packages it. The buyer can't find a second option, so you become a **category of one**. The test Alex uses is how the buyer feels: the offer should be so good that saying no feels foolish.\n\n<div class=\"formula\" markdown=\"1\">\n**Commodity** (compared on price) → **Differentiated** (compared on features) → **Grand Slam** (no comparison, priced on\nHow Alex would apply it:\n- **He diagnoses commoditisation first.** When a business complains about price pressure, he assumes the offer is comparable and fixes that before touching ads or sales scripts.\n- **He builds from the buyer's problems, not from the product.** His starting point is a long list of everything that could go wrong for the buyer, and the offer is the answer to that list.\n- **He changes the unit of sale.** He moves away from hours, sessions or seats toward an outcome with a time frame, so the price can no longer be divided and compared.\n- **He favours cheap, high-value pieces.** Templates, recordings, checklists and software raise value with little extra delivery cost, so margin grows as the offer improves.\n- **He raises the price once the value is clearly higher.** The point of the work is to charge more than competitors and still have the buyer feel they got the better deal.\n- **He keeps the core simple and enhances around it.** Scarcity, urgency, bonuses and guarantees come last, on top of a solid core.\n- **Questions he would ask:** Who exactly is this for? What could the buyer compare you with today? What are all the things that could stop them getting the result? Which of those does nobody else solve? What would it take for them to feel stupid saying no?\n\n### Starving Crowd (link: offers/starving-crowd.html)\nTerm: Starving Crowd\nCard: Pick a market that is already hungry for what you sell. The right market makes an average offer work, and the wrong one makes a great offer fail.\nKey rule: Market > Offer > Persuasion\nGist: Before you improve an offer, check who you are selling it to. If you sell food to a crowd that is starving, you don't need to be a brilliant salesperson or have the best recipe. If you sell to people who aren't hungry or can't pay, no copy and no script will save you.\n\n<div class=\"formula\" markdown=\"1\">\n**Market** > **Offer** > **Persuasion**\n</div>\n\nThis is the order of importance. Most people invest the other way round: they polish sales scripts and ads first, tweak the offer second, and almost never question the market. Alex starts at the top. A good market forgives a mediocre offer and mediocre selling. A bad market punishes even excellent\nHow Alex would apply it:\n- **He checks the market before the offer.** When someone asks why their sales are slow, his first suspicion is the market, not the copy.\n- **He wants all four criteria, and he weighs pain and money most.** Rule of thumb: a market that scores badly on pain or purchasing power is rarely worth saving with a better offer.\n- **He drills down from health, wealth or relationships** until he can name the buyer and their exact problem.\n- **He prefers buyers already paying for a bad solution.** Existing spending proves both pain and money.\n- **He prefers business buyers when he wants high prices.** Companies usually have more money and a clearer return on a solved problem than individuals.\n- **He picks one market and commits** rather than spreading the same offer across several.\n- **Questions he would ask:** Who exactly is this for? What does the problem cost them today? How are they solving it now? Who signs the cheque? Where can you find the first hundred of them? Why will this market be bigger in three years?\n\n### Commit to a Niche (link: offers/niche.html)\nTerm: Commit to a Niche\nCard: Narrow your market until the offer sounds made for one specific buyer, then stay there. A narrower offer is more relevant, more believable and can charge more.\nKey rule: Narrow the market, keep the market, iterate the offer.\nGist: A narrower market looks like fewer customers. In practice it is the opposite of a loss. \"Accounting services\" is a commodity that competes with every bookkeeper in town. \"Accounting for dental practices\" speaks to one owner's exact situation, so it sounds more relevant, feels more likely to work and supports a higher price. The product inside can be almost the same. The positioning changes what it is worth.\n\n<div class=\"formula\" markdown=\"1\">\n**Narrower market** → more relevant offer → higher perceived likelihood → **higher price**\n</div>\n\nThe second half of the idea is **commitment**. Picking a niche is easy. Staying with it long enough to learn its language, collect proof and become known is what makes it pay. When results are slow, Alex's default is to keep the market and fix the offer, not to jump to a new market and start from\nHow Alex would apply it:\n- **He narrows until the offer feels personal.** The test is whether the buyer reads it and thinks it was made for them.\n- **He raises the price after narrowing.** A specific offer is worth more, so he expects to charge more for the same core work.\n- **He keeps the market and changes the offer.** Weak results are a reason to rework the promise, price, guarantee or bonuses, not to start over in a new market.\n- **He uses the niche to build reusable assets** so that delivery cost falls with every new client.\n- **He widens only after winning.** Expansion goes to adjacent markets once the first one is clearly working.\n- **Questions he would ask:** Who is the one buyer this is for? What do you know about them that a generalist doesn't? How long have you actually stayed with this market? What have you changed in the offer before blaming the market?\n\n### Charge What It's Worth (link: offers/charge-what-its-worth.html)\nTerm: Charge What It's Worth (Virtuous Cycle of Price)\nCard: Don't compete on price. A premium price funds better delivery, attracts more committed clients and produces better results, which justifies the price again.\nKey rule: Higher price → more margin and more committed buyers → better results → proof that justifies the price\nGist: Being the cheapest is a weak position. There is always someone willing to go lower, and a low price leaves no money to do the job well. Alex argues the opposite: charge a premium, and use the margin to deliver a result so good that the price feels small next to it.\n\n<div class=\"formula\" markdown=\"1\">\n**Higher price** → more margin and more committed buyers → **better results** → proof and referrals → **justifies a higher price**\n</div>\n\nThis is the **virtuous cycle of price**. The same loop runs the other way at a low price: thin margins, rushed delivery, clients who care less, weak results, no proof and more pressure to cut. The condition is that value stays well above the price. A high price without value is not premium, it is just\nHow Alex would apply it:\n- **He refuses to compete on being cheapest.** He sees no lasting advantage in a race to the bottom.\n- **He raises value first, then raises the price.** Niche, Grand Slam Offer and guarantees come before the higher number.\n- **He reinvests the margin** in better delivery and more marketing spend per client, which a cheaper competitor cannot match.\n- **He wants buyers who are invested.** He treats a higher price as a filter that improves client behaviour and results.\n- **He tests price increases on new prospects** and judges them by total profit, not by close rate alone.\n- **He changes the unit of sale** from hours or sessions to an outcome, so the price is compared with the value, not with other vendors.\n- **Questions he would ask:** What is the result worth to the buyer in money? Who is the most expensive competitor, and why are you below them? What is your close rate? If almost everyone says yes, why haven't you raised the price? What would you do for clients with twice the margin?\n\n### The Value Equation (link: offers/value-equation.html)\nTerm: Value Equation\nCard: How a buyer decides, in their head, whether something is worth its price. Push two levers up and two down.\nKey rule: Value = (Dream Outcome × Perceived Likelihood) ÷ (Time Delay × Effort & Sacrifice)\nGist: People don't buy a product, they buy a **change**. An offer is worth as much as that change is big and believable, divided by how long it takes and how much pain it costs to get there.\n\n<div class=\"formula\" markdown=\"1\">\n**Value** = (**Dream Outcome** × **Perceived Likelihood of Achievement**) ÷ (**Time Delay** × **Effort & Sacrifice**)\n</div>\n\nMost sellers only compete on the top line: a bigger promise. The bigger opportunity is usually the bottom line. If the time to a result and the work for the buyer get close to zero, value grows very large, and so does what you can\nHow Alex would apply it:\n- **He starts from the bottom of the fraction.** His first question is rarely \"how do we promise more?\" but \"how do we make this faster and easier for them?\"\n- **He looks for the fastest felt win** and builds the first week of delivery around it, because early proof lowers refunds and raises referrals.\n- **He sells the outcome in the buyer's words** and moves the mechanics (hours, deliverables, process) to the back.\n- **He raises likelihood with proof before he raises the promise:** case studies, specific numbers, a narrower target customer, and a guarantee.\n- **He prices from value, not cost.** When value goes up on all four levers, he expects the price to go up too.\n- **Questions he would ask:** What does the client really want, and how will others see them when they get it? What would make a sceptic believe it? What can they get in week one? What do they have to do that you could do for them?\n\n### Problems into Solutions (link: offers/problems-solutions.html)\nTerm: Problems & Solutions\nCard: List every obstacle the buyer will hit before, during and after buying, then turn each one into a \"how to\" solution. The longer the list, the more value you can build.\nKey rule: Every problem the buyer has is a solution you can sell: obstacle → how to get X even if / without Y.\nGist: Once you know the dream outcome, the next question is: what stands between the buyer and that result? Every answer is an obstacle, and every obstacle is a piece of value you can add to the offer. Most sellers solve only the core problem (the dashboard, the workout, the filling) and leave the buyer alone with everything around it.\n\n<div class=\"formula\" markdown=\"1\">\n**Dream outcome** → list **every obstacle** (before, during, after) → rewrite each as **\"how to get X even if / without Y\"**\n</div>\n\nThe list is the raw material for the whole offer. The obstacles you find become solutions, the solutions get delivery vehicles, and the best of those get stacked. Competitors who never wrote the list can't match what you build from\nHow Alex would apply it:\n- **He treats every objection as a missing piece of the offer.** If buyers keep saying the same thing on sales calls, the offer needs a component that solves it.\n- **He walks the timeline literally:** what happens the minute after they pay, the first week, the first time they get stuck, the day after the result. He writes it all down.\n- **He mines real language** from sales calls, support tickets and reviews instead of guessing what buyers think.\n- **He pays special attention to \"I can't do it\".** Belief in the method is not enough if the buyer doubts themselves, so he looks for ways to make the buyer's part smaller.\n- **He wants a long list first and judgement later.** Filtering happens in trim and stack, never while brainstorming.\n- **Questions he would ask:** What does the buyer worry about the night before signing? Where do clients usually get stuck or quit? What do they ask you most often? What happens after you leave? Which of these does a competitor already solve?\n\n### Delivery Vehicles (link: offers/delivery-vehicles.html)\nTerm: Delivery Vehicles (Solutions → Delivery)\nCard: The same solution can be delivered in many ways, and each way has a different value to the buyer and a different cost to you. Brainstorm many, then pick on value versus cost.\nKey rule: For each solution: who gets attention (1:1, group, 1:many) × who does the work (DIY, DWY, DFY) × medium × speed and access\nGist: A solution on your list (\"how to answer new questions yourself in under an hour\") says nothing about how the buyer will get it. It could be a personal session, a recorded video, a template, a support chat or a piece of software. Each of these is a delivery vehicle, and the choice changes both what the buyer feels they got and what it costs you to provide.\n\n<div class=\"formula\" markdown=\"1\">\n**Delivery vehicle** = **attention** (1-on-1 · small group · one-to-many) × **who does the work** (do-it-yourself · done-with-you · done-for-you) × **medium** × **speed & access**\n</div>\n\nThe goal is to generate many options for each solution and only then judge them. The best offers mix a few expensive, high-touch pieces where they matter most with many cheap, scalable pieces that still carry high\nHow Alex would apply it:\n- **He writes every combination down first** and only then scores value against cost. The scoring belongs to the trim-and-stack step.\n- **He looks for things that cost once and deliver many times:** recordings, templates, checklists, software. These are where margin comes from.\n- **He spends expensive, personal delivery where it changes the outcome most,** typically at the start (to get a fast win) and at the points where clients usually get stuck.\n- **He uses DFY as a lever for price.** Removing effort is one of the strongest value moves, so the done-for-you pieces often anchor the premium version.\n- **He matches access to risk:** fast response where a stuck client would quit, slower channels everywhere else.\n- **He uses the ten-times-more and ten-times-less questions** to break out of habitual delivery.\n- **Questions he would ask:** Where in the journey does the client most need a human? What could be recorded once and reused? What would make them feel supported without costing you hours? If you had to serve ten times more clients next year, which pieces would break?\n\n### Trim & Stack (link: offers/trim-and-stack.html)\nTerm: Trim & Stack\nCard: Score every solution and delivery option by value to the buyer and cost to you, cut the costly low-value ones, and stack what remains into one offer whose total value clearly exceeds the price.\nKey rule: Keep low cost / high value, keep the critical high cost / high value, cut high cost / low value, then stack and show the total.\nGist: After listing problems and brainstorming delivery vehicles, you have far more possible components than any offer should contain. Trim and stack is the editing step. You judge every combination of solution and delivery on two things only: how much the buyer values it and how much it costs you to deliver.\n\n<div class=\"formula\" markdown=\"1\">\n**Trim:** cut high cost / low value · keep low cost / high value · keep the few critical high cost / high value pieces\n\n**Stack:** bundle what remains, name and value each piece, show the **total value** next to the **price**\n</div>\n\nThe result is one offer made of many named components. When the buyer sees each piece with its own value, the sum is much larger than the price, and comparing it with a competitor's single line item stops making\nHow Alex would apply it:\n- **He cuts before he adds.** His first pass looks for what costs the most and moves the outcome least, because that improves margin and value at the same time.\n- **He hunts for low cost / high value pieces** and multiplies them: templates, recordings, scripts, checklists, software.\n- **He protects the pieces the outcome depends on,** even when they are expensive, and raises the price to fund them instead of removing them.\n- **He moves expensive extras into a higher tier** rather than giving them to everyone.\n- **He presents the stack in the buyer's order of concern** and names each piece after the result it delivers.\n- **He wants a clear gap between total value and price,** as a rule of thumb large enough that the price feels small next to it, but every number must be believable.\n- **Questions he would ask:** What do you deliver today that clients never mention? Which piece costs you the most hours? Which cheap piece do clients thank you for? If you removed one component, which would they miss? Does every major obstacle still have a solution in the stack?\n\n### Scarcity (link: offers/scarcity.html)\nTerm: Scarcity\nCard: Limiting how many people can get the offer makes it more desirable. In B2B your capacity is already limited, so stating it honestly is both true and persuasive.\nKey rule: Limit the quantity, not the time: total cap, growth cap (N new per period) or cohort cap. It must be real.\nGist: People want more of what they can't easily get. When an offer is available to anyone at any time, there is no cost to waiting, so buyers wait. When only a few spots exist, saying \"maybe later\" carries the risk of losing the chance, and the fear of missing out pushes the decision forward.\n\n<div class=\"formula\" markdown=\"1\">\n**Scarcity** = a limit on **quantity** (how many can buy)\n\n**Urgency** = a limit on **time** (until when they can buy)\n</div>\n\nScarcity raises demand without changing what you deliver. It only works over time if it is real: if the \"last three spots\" are still open next month, buyers learn to ignore you. In a service business this is rarely a problem, because your hours, team and attention are genuinely\nHow Alex would apply it:\n- **He starts from real capacity.** The first question is how many clients the business can serve without results dropping, and the limit follows from that.\n- **He prefers the growth limit for services,** because it reflects onboarding reality and can be used every month without wearing out.\n- **He states the reason and the remaining count openly,** and treats the waiting list as proof of demand that also justifies raising prices.\n- **He uses scarcity as a pricing signal:** when spots consistently sell out, the right move is a higher price, not more spots squeezed in.\n- **He combines it with urgency** (cohorts with start dates, bonuses for the first few) to give buyers both a reason to want it and a reason to act now.\n- **He refuses fake limits,** because his business depends on long-term reputation and referrals more than on one more sale.\n- **Questions he would ask:** How many new clients can you onboard each month without delivery suffering? What happens today when several sign at once? Do you have a waiting list? When did you last turn someone away? Are you full often enough that the price should go up?\n\n### Urgency (link: offers/urgency.html)\nTerm: Urgency\nCard: A real time limit on your offer, price or bonus. It gives buyers a reason to decide now instead of drifting into \"maybe later\".\nKey rule: Scarcity limits how many. Urgency limits how long.\nGist: Most lost sales end with \"not now\", and \"not now\" rarely comes back. Urgency fixes this by putting a clock on the decision: the offer, the price or a bonus is only available until a set moment. When a buyer knows that waiting costs them something, they decide.\n\n<div class=\"formula\" markdown=\"1\">\n**Scarcity** = limited **quantity** (seats, units, slots) · **Urgency** = limited **time** (dates, windows, deadlines)\n</div>\n\nUrgency only works if it is honest. A deadline you move or a countdown that resets teaches buyers to ignore you. The best urgency already exists: you find the real deadlines that already exist in your business and in the buyer's world, and you make them\nHow Alex would apply it:\n- **He uses urgency on almost every offer**, because without a reason to act now, most buyers will postpone even when they want it.\n- **He prefers rolling structures:** cohorts and seasonal promotions that renew themselves, so there is always a fresh, honest deadline and he never has to invent one.\n- **He puts the deadline on the bonus before the price.** The buyer who waits still can buy, they just lose the extra. It drives decisions without making late buyers feel punished.\n- **He combines urgency with scarcity** where both are real: limited spots in a group that starts on a date.\n- **He refuses fake urgency.** His rule of thumb is that the long-term cost of being caught lying is far bigger than the short-term sales it brings.\n- **He states the cost of waiting in plain terms:** what the buyer loses each week they delay, and when the next chance comes.\n- **Questions he would ask:** What real deadline already exists here? When does the next group or slot start? What happens to the buyer's problem if they wait three months? Which bonus could expire without hurting the core offer? Have you ever broken a deadline, and do your buyers know it?\n\n### Bonuses (link: offers/bonuses.html)\nTerm: Bonuses\nCard: Extras added to the core offer, each built to remove one specific reason the buyer might not buy. Done well, the bonuses alone are worth more than the price.\nKey rule: One obstacle, one bonus, one name, one stated value. Total bonus value greater than the core offer.\nGist: A bonus is a sales tool with one job. Every hesitation a buyer has (\"my team won't use it\", \"I don't have time\", \"what if it breaks\") is a problem you can solve with a separate, named piece of the offer. When each worry has its own answer, the buyer runs out of reasons to say no.\n\n<div class=\"formula\" markdown=\"1\">\n**Obstacle** → **bonus that solves it** → **name** + **stated value** · Sum of bonus values > price of the core offer\n</div>\n\nBonuses also change how the offer is perceived. One offer with ten features is one thing to evaluate. One core offer followed by five bonuses, each with its own value, feels like the buyer gets far more than they pay. The same contents, split and presented this way, are worth more in the buyer's\nHow Alex would apply it:\n- **He builds bonuses from objections.** He collects the reasons people gave for not buying and turns each one into a bonus.\n- **He stacks many small bonuses** rather than one big one, and presents them one by one after the price.\n- **He makes the bonuses worth more than the core.** His rule of thumb is that the buyer should feel the bonuses alone justify the purchase.\n- **He favours bonuses with low delivery cost and high perceived value**, such as templates, scripts, recordings and tools that save the buyer time.\n- **He uses partners for bonuses** because it adds real value without cost, and it builds relationships that can send leads back.\n- **He attaches urgency or scarcity to bonuses** instead of discounting the core price.\n- **Questions he would ask:** Why did the last ten people who didn't buy say no? Which of those reasons does your core offer not address? What do you already have that solves it? What would each bonus cost the buyer to get elsewhere? Which partner already sells something your buyers need?\n\n### Guarantees (link: offers/guarantees.html)\nTerm: Guarantees (Risk Reversal)\nCard: A promise that moves the risk of buying from the buyer to the seller. It raises perceived likelihood of success and usually wins more sales than it costs in refunds.\nKey rule: If you do X and don't get Y, we will do Z. Conditions are the actions that create success.\nGist: Every purchase carries risk, and by default the buyer holds all of it. They pay first and hope it works. A guarantee flips that. The seller takes on the risk, so the buyer's biggest fear (paying and getting nothing) is gone. This raises the perceived likelihood of achievement in the value equation, which raises value directly.\n\n<div class=\"formula\" markdown=\"1\">\n**Strong guarantee** = If you do **X** (the actions that create success) and do not get **Y** (the specific result) by **when**, we will do **Z** (refund, extra work, payment back).\n</div>\n\nMost sellers fear that guarantees will be abused. In practice the extra buyers who say yes usually outnumber those who ask for refunds. And if a guarantee would bankrupt you, that tells you more about your delivery than about the\nHow Alex would apply it:\n- **He wants a guarantee on almost every offer**, and usually the strongest one the business can survive.\n- **He ties conditions to the behaviours that create success**, so the guarantee doubles as a way to make clients do the work.\n- **He prefers service guarantees** (\"we keep working until you get it\") when a refund would hurt, because they cost less and keep the client.\n- **He stacks guarantees** to remove several fears at once, and gives each one a name.\n- **He treats refund rates as a delivery signal.** If a guarantee would be claimed often, he fixes the product before he weakens the guarantee.\n- **He uses performance deals** when results are measurable, because they make the decision almost risk-free for the buyer and can pay the seller more.\n- **Questions he would ask:** What is the buyer's biggest fear about buying? What must they do to succeed, and could that be your condition? What would happen if 10% asked for their money back? Can you promise more work instead of money? What would you name it so people remember it?\n\n### Naming (MAGIC) (link: offers/naming.html)\nTerm: MAGIC Naming Formula\nCard: A checklist for naming an offer so the name alone tells the right buyer that it is for them and what they will get. A good name attracts better leads and lets you refresh the same offer when ads get tired.\nKey rule: MAGIC = Magnetic reason why, Announce the avatar, Give them a goal, Indicate a time interval, Complete with a container word\nGist: The name is the first part of the offer a buyer sees, often before any price or detail. A generic name (\"Consulting Package B\", \"Monthly Membership\") puts you back in the commodity trap, because it looks like everything else. A specific name tells the right person, at a glance, that this was made for them and what it will do.\n\n<div class=\"formula\" markdown=\"1\">\n**M**agnetic reason why · **A**nnounce the avatar · **G**ive them a goal · **I**ndicate a time interval · **C**omplete with a container word\n</div>\n\nMAGIC is a checklist. You do not need every element. Most strong names use three to five of the five. Names also wear out. The same core offer can be renamed and repackaged for a new season or a new audience, which keeps ads and outreach feeling fresh without rebuilding the\nHow Alex would apply it:\n- **He treats the name as part of the offer**, because it decides who stops to look at all.\n- **He leads with the avatar and the goal**, since those two elements do most of the filtering and attracting.\n- **He uses the reason why to create a natural deadline**, so naming and urgency work together.\n- **He drafts many names and picks the one buyers would repeat** to a friend or colleague, not the one that sounds clever.\n- **He renames instead of rebuilding.** When ads fatigue, his first move is a new name and angle on the same delivery.\n- **He checks platform and legal rules** before putting a specific claim in a name, and adjusts wording to stay compliant.\n- **Questions he would ask:** Who exactly is this for? What result do they want, in their words? Why is this being offered now? How long does it take? What would you call it if you had to fit it in a headline? How long has the current name been running?\n\n## BOOK: $100M Leads (How to get strangers to want to buy your stuff)\nLink: leads/\n\n### Leads & Engaged Leads (link: leads/engaged-leads.html)\nTerm: Engaged Leads\nCard: A lead is someone you can contact. An engaged lead is someone who has shown interest in what you sell, and those are the only leads that turn into customers.\nKey rule: Cost per engaged lead = what you spent (time or money) ÷ number of engaged leads\nGist: A **lead** is a person you can get in touch with: a name with a phone number, an email, a LinkedIn profile. An **engaged lead** is a lead who has done something that shows interest in your stuff. They replied, booked a call, downloaded a lead magnet, came to an event, or asked a question. The difference is action. A list of 5,000 emails is a list of leads. The 40 people who answered your message are engaged leads.\n\n**Advertising** simply means making something known. A text to an old colleague, a LinkedIn post, a cold email and a paid ad are all advertising. The point of all of it is the same: turn strangers into engaged leads, then turn engaged leads into customers in a conversation.\n\n<div class=\"formula\" markdown=\"1\">\n**Strangers** → (advertising) → **Leads** → (they show interest) → **Engaged leads** → (sales) → **Customers**\n</div>\n\nEvery engaged lead is paid for, either with **time** (writing messages, making content) or with **money** (ads, agencies, list data). So the number that matters is the **cost per engaged lead**, and the business grows when you get more of them, cheaper, from sources you\nHow Alex would apply it:\n- **He asks for the number of engaged leads first.** Before talking about sales skill or pricing, he wants to know how many people raised their hand last month. Too few is the most common reason a small business isn't growing.\n- **He treats lead flow as the default bottleneck** for businesses that sell well once they're in a conversation. More engaged leads is usually the fastest fix.\n- **He prices time.** If you spend your evenings on outreach, he counts those hours as a cost and expects you to know what each engaged lead costs you.\n- **He compares cost to value.** If an engaged lead is clearly worth more than it costs, his answer is to do more, a lot more, before trying anything new.\n- **He wants sources you control.** Word of mouth and luck are fine, but he builds at least one channel you can turn up on demand.\n- **Questions he would ask:** How many engaged leads did you get last month, and from where? What counts as engaged for you? What did each one cost in time or money? How many became customers? What is a customer worth to you?\n\n### Lead Magnets (link: leads/lead-magnets.html)\nTerm: Lead Magnet\nCard: A free offer that completely solves one narrow problem and, in doing so, reveals the next problem your paid offer solves. It turns strangers into engaged leads who already trust you.\nKey rule: Lead magnet = complete solution to a narrow problem that reveals the next problem\nGist: A **lead magnet** is a free offer that gives a complete solution to a narrow problem. Solving it reveals a **next problem**, and that next problem is the one your core offer solves. It is a small, real win, not a brochure or \"an ebook with 50 tips\". The person gets value, and you get an engaged lead who already trusts you and knows what they need next.\n\n<div class=\"formula\" markdown=\"1\">\n**Narrow problem** solved for free → reveals the **next problem** → solved by your **core offer**\n</div>\n\nThe lead magnet is how you make every channel work better. A message, post or ad that offers something valuable and free gets far more responses than one that asks for a purchase. The quality of what you give away also sets what people expect from what you\nHow Alex would apply it:\n- **He works backwards from the core offer.** First he asks what problem the paid offer solves, then which problem comes right before it. That earlier problem is the lead magnet.\n- **He raises the bar on quality.** His rule of thumb: if people wouldn't pay for it, it isn't good enough to give away.\n- **He prefers the cheapest delivery that still impresses.** Software and recorded information scale for free; a service converts better but costs time. He picks based on volume and margin.\n- **He tests names and hooks before polishing.** The same magnet with a better name can get many more takers, so he tests several and keeps the winner.\n- **He puts the lead magnet in every channel.** Warm messages, posts, cold emails and ads all offer the same free thing, so every channel produces engaged leads instead of asking for a sale.\n- **He cuts anything that slows consumption:** long intros, extra form fields, downloads that need setup.\n- **Questions he would ask:** What problem does your paid offer solve, and what problem comes just before it? Would anyone pay for your free thing? How long does it take to consume? What do they find out at the end that makes them want more? What exactly do you ask them to do next?\n\n### The Core Four (link: leads/core-four.html)\nTerm: Core Four\nCard: There are only four ways to let people know about you, set by two questions. Do they already know you, and are you reaching one person or many? Pick one, master it, then add the next.\nKey rule: Known × one-to-one = warm outreach · known × one-to-many = free content · unknown × one-to-one = cold outreach · unknown × one-to-many = paid ads\nGist: Every way of getting engaged leads is a version of four activities. You can only reach people who **know you** or people who **don't**, and you can reach them **one at a time** or **many at once**. Those two axes give four combinations, and Alex calls them the **Core Four**.\n\n<div class=\"formula\" markdown=\"1\">\n| | **One-to-one** | **One-to-many** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Known audience** (warm) | [Warm outreach](warm-outreach.html) | [Posting free content](free-content.html) |\n| **Unknown audience** (cold) | [Cold outreach](cold-outreach.html) | [Paid ads](paid-ads.html) |\n</div>\n\nSeeing it this way stops the hunt for a secret channel. LinkedIn, email, events, podcasts and billboards are all one of these four. The question becomes which one you do, how much of it, and how well. The rule is simple: **start with one, get good at it, then add the\nHow Alex would apply it:\n- **He maps what you're doing onto the four.** Whatever tactics you list, he sorts them into the grid. Usually it shows you're barely doing any of them.\n- **He picks one channel based on resources.** Little money means you buy leads with time: warm outreach, content or cold outreach. Money and no time means ads.\n- **He asks about volume before tactics.** If the channel \"doesn't work\", his first suspicion is that you didn't do enough of it.\n- **He adds channels only when the current one is predictable,** and treats each new one as its own skill to learn.\n- **He keeps the same lead magnet across channels,** so what wins in one channel can be reused in the next.\n- **He cuts shiny-channel hopping.** A new platform is still one of the four. He would rather you get better at the one you have.\n- **Questions he would ask:** Which of the four are you doing now, and how much each day? Do you have more time or more money? How many engaged leads did your main channel bring last month? Is that channel predictable yet? What is your offer in every message?\n\n### Warm Outreach (link: leads/warm-outreach.html)\nTerm: Warm Outreach\nCard: Personal, one-to-one messages to people who already know you. It's free, works today and is where almost everyone should start getting engaged leads.\nKey rule: ACA = Acknowledge, Compliment, Ask. Reach out to 100 people a day.\nGist: **Warm outreach** is reaching out, one person at a time, to people who already know you: friends, former colleagues, past clients, classmates, people in your phone. It's the top-left box of the [Core Four](core-four.html): known audience, one-to-one. It costs no money, the reply rate is the highest of the four channels, and you can start in the next hour.\n\nThe method is simple. Go through every contact list you have. Send each person a personal message. Start a real conversation before you mention what you do. Keep it going with **ACA**. Then make an offer, or ask whether they know anyone who could use it. Do this at volume and follow up.\n\n<div class=\"formula\" markdown=\"1\">\n**Contact list** → personal message → conversation (**A**cknowledge, **C**ompliment, **A**sk) → offer or referral ask → follow up\n</div>\n\nWarm outreach also teaches you to sell. You hear real objections, real words for the problem and real reactions to your offer, from people who are kind enough to answer. That makes every other channel better\nHow Alex would apply it:\n- **He makes it the default first channel.** For anyone with no money and no leads, he would start here before content, cold outreach or ads.\n- **He sets a daily number and holds to it.** His rule of thumb is 100 reach-outs a day, every day, until it's a habit and the numbers are clear.\n- **He leads with value, not a pitch.** He'd offer the lead magnet or the service itself for free to the first people, to get proof and practice.\n- **He uses the referral ask** because it removes pressure and opens doors to people you don't know yet.\n- **He follows up more than feels comfortable,** because most replies come after the first message.\n- **He treats it as sales training.** Every conversation tells you how people describe the problem and what they object to. He'd feed that back into the offer.\n- **Questions he would ask:** How many people are on your combined contact lists? How many did you message today? What's your reply rate? What do you offer them for free? Are you asking for referrals? Who did you follow up with this week?\n\n### Posting Free Content (link: leads/free-content.html)\nTerm: Posting Free Content\nCard: Publishing valuable content to people who already follow you, so that trust builds and warm leads come to you. One-to-many to people who know you.\nKey rule: Every piece = Hook + Retain + Reward. Give far more than you ask.\nGist: Free content is the Core Four channel where you speak to many people at once who already know you: followers, subscribers, readers. You give value in public, they consume it, and over time some of them raise their hand and ask to buy. It is slow to start and compounds, because each piece keeps working long after you post it.\n\n<div class=\"formula\" markdown=\"1\">\n**Content that gets leads** = **Hook** (they start) + **Retain** (they stay) + **Reward** (they got value, so they come back) + **occasional asks** (they know what to do next)\n</div>\n\nThe core rule is to give far more than you ask. Most pieces should only give. When you do ask, the ask is clear and specific, and because you rarely ask, people listen. The best raw material is your own real work: what you built, fixed, learned or got wrong this\nHow Alex would apply it:\n- **He treats content as a long game.** He expects it to compound over months and years, and he does not judge it on a few weeks of results.\n- **He leans hard on giving.** He would rather give away his best ideas for free, because the people who act on them become the best buyers.\n- **He builds every piece on Hook, Retain, Reward.** If a piece underperforms, he checks which of the three failed instead of blaming the algorithm.\n- **He documents instead of inventing.** He turns what he and his companies actually do into content, which keeps it honest and credible.\n- **He picks one platform, maxes it out, then goes wide.** He repurposes pieces that already worked rather than starting from scratch elsewhere.\n- **He pushes volume first, then improves.** More output, then better output, then new platforms, in that order.\n- **Questions he would ask:** Who exactly is this for, and what do they care about right now? What did you do this week that they would want to see? What is the hook, what keeps them, and what do they walk away with? How often do you ask, and what exactly do you ask them to do? How many pieces have you posted on this platform so far?\n\n### Cold Outreach (link: leads/cold-outreach.html)\nTerm: Cold Outreach\nCard: Contacting strangers one by one, by email, DM, phone or text. Response rates are low, so it works through targeted lists, a big free offer, many follow-ups and volume.\nKey rule: Targeted list + personal first line + big fast value + many touches, repeated at volume\nGist: Cold outreach is the Core Four channel where you contact people who do not know you, one at a time. Its big advantage is control: you choose exactly who hears from you, and you are not limited by how many people you already know. Its big drawback is that strangers ignore most messages, so it only works with volume, persistence and an offer that makes replying worth it.\n\n<div class=\"formula\" markdown=\"1\">\n**Engaged leads from cold outreach** = **Right people** (list) × **Reason to reply** (personalisation + big fast value) × **Touches** (follow-ups) × **Volume**\n</div>\n\nStrangers have no reason to trust you. You earn a reply by showing you did your homework and by offering something useful for free, often a lead magnet. You do not sell in the first message. You get an engaged lead first, and the sale happens later in\nHow Alex would apply it:\n- **He fixes the list first.** If replies are low, he checks whether the right people are being contacted before touching the copy.\n- **He makes the offer so good that ignoring it feels like a loss.** A big, fast, free result beats a request for a meeting.\n- **He expects most results from follow-ups.** He plans several touches before the first message goes out.\n- **He does it by hand, then automates, then hires.** He proves the message works himself, then uses software and people to scale it.\n- **He treats it as volume plus testing.** He sets a daily number of contacts, sticks to it, and improves one variable at a time.\n- **He uses cold outreach to buy speed when warm contacts run out,** and he keeps the personal tone even at high volume.\n- **Questions he would ask:** Who exactly is on the list, and how did you choose them? What do you offer in the first message that is worth a reply? How many touches does each person get? How many messages do you send per day, and what is the reply rate? What did you change last week, and what happened?\n\n### Paid Ads (link: leads/paid-ads.html)\nTerm: Paid Ads\nCard: Paying a platform to show your message to many strangers at once. The fastest way to scale leads, but only once your offer works and your money model can pay back the cost of each customer.\nKey rule: Ad = Callout + Value + Call to Action. Start small, find a winner, scale.\nGist: Paid ads are the Core Four channel where you reach many people who do not know you, by paying a platform for their attention. Of the four channels it scales the fastest: once you find an ad that works, more money buys more leads. It is also the channel where mistakes cost the most, because a bad ad simply burns budget faster.\n\n<div class=\"formula\" markdown=\"1\">\n**Ad** = **Callout** (who it is for) + **Value** (what they get) + **Call to Action** (what to do now)\n</div>\n\nAn ad is a hook attached to an offer. The hook gets the right stranger to stop. The offer, often a lead magnet, gives them a reason to act. Success is measured in money: what one engaged lead costs, what one customer costs, and whether your money model earns that back fast enough to keep buying\nHow Alex would apply it:\n- **He wants a proven offer before paid traffic.** He gets results with warm outreach or content first, then pours money on what already converts.\n- **He tests many hooks against one offer.** He expects most creatives to fail and treats finding the winners as the job.\n- **He judges ads on customer economics.** His question is whether the customer pays back the ad cost fast, ideally inside the first 30 days.\n- **He fixes the money model to afford more ads.** Upsells and a stronger front-end offer let him outspend competitors on acquisition.\n- **He scales winners step by step,** watching cost per customer, and cuts anything that does not pay back.\n- **He insists on fast follow-up,** because a paid lead that waits goes cold.\n- **Questions he would ask:** Who is the ad for, and does the first line say so? What do they get for responding? What does one engaged lead cost, and what does one customer cost? How much gross profit does a customer bring in the first 30 days? How many variants have you tested, and which one won?\n\n### Lead Getters (link: leads/lead-getters.html)\nTerm: Lead Getters\nCard: Other people who bring you leads: customers, employees, agencies, and affiliates and partners. They turn the Core Four from something you do into something that runs without your time per lead.\nKey rule: Customers refer, employees run the Core Four, agencies teach, affiliates lend their audience\nGist: You can run the Core Four yourself: warm outreach, free content, cold outreach and paid ads. But your time is limited, and at some point you are the bottleneck. Lead getters are the people who do the advertising for you or send you their audience. They are how a business keeps getting leads when the founder stops doing it all.\n\n<div class=\"formula\" markdown=\"1\">\n**Lead getters** = **Customers** (referrals) + **Employees** (run the Core Four) + **Agencies** (rent skills) + **Affiliates & partners** (other people's audiences)\n</div>\n\nEach type needs a different incentive and a different setup. Customers refer when they got great results and it is easy to do. Employees need a process they can follow. Agencies are best used to learn a skill. Affiliates and partners need something that helps their audience and a fair reward for sending it to\nHow Alex would apply it:\n- **He makes delivery referral-worthy first.** He sees referrals as a result of great outcomes, and fixes the product before building a referral programme.\n- **He builds the ask into the process.** Asking for referrals happens at a fixed point, right after the customer feels the win, every time.\n- **He does it, documents it, then delegates it.** No activity goes to an employee before he has proven it works and written down how.\n- **He hires agencies to learn, then brings skills in-house.** He keeps accounts and data in his own name and treats the agency fee as tuition.\n- **He goes where the audience already is.** Partners and affiliates with his ideal customers are the fastest way to reach many people who will trust him.\n- **He judges every lead getter on numbers.** Engaged leads, customers and cost per customer by source, so he knows which to grow.\n- **Questions he would ask:** Do your customers get results good enough to talk about? When do you ask for referrals, and what do you hand them? Which lead-getting activity have you proven and documented well enough to hand over? What are you learning from your agency? Who already has your customers, and what would help their audience?\n\n### The Rule of 100 (link: leads/rule-of-100.html)\nTerm: Rule of 100\nCard: Do 100 primary lead-getting actions every day for 100 days. Early on, volume teaches you more than talent or planning ever will.\nKey rule: 100 primary actions a day × 100 days, done first, tracked daily\nGist: Most new businesses don't fail because they picked the wrong channel. They fail because they never did enough of anything to find out. The Rule of 100 fixes that with a simple commitment: every day, for 100 days, do 100 of your primary lead-getting actions.\n\n<div class=\"formula\" markdown=\"1\">\n**100 primary actions a day** × **100 days**, done **first**, tracked **daily**\n</div>\n\nWhat counts as \"100\" depends on the channel. In outreach it is 100 reach-outs. In content it is 100 minutes spent making and posting. In paid ads it is commonly described as $100 a day. The number is a rule of thumb. It is big enough to produce real data and concrete enough that you can't negotiate your way out of\nHow Alex would apply it:\n- **He diagnoses volume before anything else.** When someone says a channel doesn't work, his first move is to ask how much they actually did. Usually the answer is far too little to judge.\n- **He insists on one channel at a time.** Spreading effort across four channels at low volume means none of them gets enough attempts to work.\n- **He makes it non-negotiable and first.** The lead-getting block goes at the start of the day and is protected like a client meeting.\n- **He treats the first 100 days as paid learning.** Poor early results are expected. The goal of this phase is enough data to see what works.\n- **He would rather see bad work at high volume than good work at low volume.** Volume can be improved later. Low volume gives you nothing to improve.\n- **He wants the numbers written down daily,** because the next step (More, Better or New) depends entirely on them.\n- **Questions he would ask:** How many reach-outs did you do yesterday, exactly? What's your daily 100, and when in the day does it happen? Which one channel are you committing to? What do your numbers show after the first two weeks? What would you drop to make room for this?\n\n### More, Better, New (link: leads/more-better-new.html)\nTerm: More, Better, New\nCard: The order in which to grow lead flow. Do more of what works, then make it work better, and only then add something new.\nKey rule: More → Better → New, in that order\nGist: Once a channel starts producing leads, there are three ways to get more out of it. You can do **more** of it, do it **better**, or try something **new**. The order matters. Each step is more expensive and less certain than the one before, so you only move on when the previous one has stopped paying.\n\n<div class=\"formula\" markdown=\"1\">\n**More** (raise volume) → **Better** (raise conversion) → **New** (add channels, platforms, offers)\n</div>\n\nMost founders get the order backwards. They get bored with what works and jump to a new platform, a new funnel or a new offer, which puts them back at zero. Shiny new things feel like progress. Usually they are a way to avoid the repetitive work that is actually producing\nHow Alex would apply it:\n- **His default answer to \"what should I do next?\" is more.** He first asks whether the current activity can simply be doubled, and usually it can.\n- **He sees boredom as the enemy of growth.** Many owners abandon what works because it's repetitive. He keeps the boring thing going and often scales it further.\n- **He moves to Better only when volume stalls,** and then works on the weakest step in the funnel, one change at a time.\n- **He is sceptical of new platforms, funnels and offers** while an existing channel still has room to grow. New comes last.\n- **He buys volume with people before he buys novelty.** Hiring someone to do more of the proven work comes before adding a channel.\n- **When he does go New, he restarts the Rule of 100** on it and keeps the old channel running.\n- **Questions he would ask:** What's working right now? Can you do twice as much of it next month? What happens to results per unit as you scale? Which step in your funnel converts worst? What's the one change you'll test there first? Why do you want to add something new, and what will it cost the channel that works?\n\n## BOOK: $100M Money Models (How to make money)\nLink: money/\n\n### Money Models (link: money/money-models.html)\nTerm: Money Model\nCard: A deliberate sequence of offers designed to make more money from each customer, faster. The business that can afford to spend the most to acquire a customer wins.\nKey rule: Attraction → Upsell → Downsell → Continuity\nGist: Most businesses have one offer and hope the customer comes back. A **money model** is a planned sequence of offers: what you sell first, what you offer right after a yes, what you offer after a no, and what keeps paying every month. Its job is to make more money from each customer, and to make it sooner.\n\n<div class=\"formula\" markdown=\"1\">\n**Attraction** (get them in) → **Upsell** (more, right after yes) → **Downsell** (save the sale after no) → **Continuity** (recurring)\n</div>\n\nThis matters because acquisition is where businesses compete. If each customer is worth more to you in the first weeks, you can spend more to get them than your competitors can. A small, simple business with a strong money model can outspend a much bigger one on ads and sales, and still grow on its own\nHow Alex would apply it:\n- **He looks at the money model before the marketing.** When growth stalls because ads seem too expensive, he first asks how much each customer is worth in the first 30 days. Often the fix is the sequence of offers.\n- **He builds it one offer at a time,** starting with the attraction offer. Once that reliably brings in the right customers, he adds the upsell, then the downsell, then continuity.\n- **He wants every customer to hear the next offer.** An upsell after every yes and a downsell after every no is a fixed step in the process.\n- **He keeps it simple.** Many offers around one core product beat a catalogue. He would cut products before he would cut offers.\n- **He measures it with 30-day gross profit against CAC,** and aims for the first month to pay for acquiring that customer and ideally two more.\n- **He prefers models that pay up front.** Setup fees, prepayments and first-month upsells bring cash in when the business needs it to acquire the next customer.\n- **Questions he would ask:** What's your entry offer, and does it lead naturally to your core product? What do you offer right after someone says yes? What happens to the people who say no? What do customers pay for every month? How much gross profit does a new customer bring in the first 30 days, compared with what they cost you?\n\n### Client-Financed Acquisition (link: money/client-financed-acquisition.html)\nTerm: Client-Financed Acquisition\nCard: When what a new customer pays in the first 30 days covers the cost to acquire and serve them, growth funds itself. At 2×, every customer pays for two more.\nKey rule: 30-day gross profit ≥ CAC (goal ≥ 2× CAC); LTGP:CAC ≥ 3:1 as a rule of thumb\nGist: Every business pays to get a customer before it earns from them. If the money comes back slowly, growth is limited by the cash in the bank, and growing faster means borrowing or raising money. **Client-financed acquisition** removes that limit. The customer's own payments in the first 30 days cover what it cost to acquire them and to deliver what they bought.\n\n<div class=\"formula\" markdown=\"1\">\n**30-day gross profit per customer** ≥ **CAC**, ideally **≥ 2 × CAC**\n</div>\n\nAt 1× acquisition pays for itself. At 2× each new customer funds the acquisition of two more. From there the only limits on growth are how many customers you can reach and how many you can serve. The money model is the tool for getting there: it is designed to pull gross profit into the first 30\nHow Alex would apply it:\n- **He looks at 30-day gross profit before the ad budget.** When someone says ads are too expensive, he usually concludes the customer is worth too little too late, and fixes that first.\n- **He targets 2× in 30 days** and treats anything below 1× as a business that can only grow with outside money.\n- **He pulls cash forward:** setup fees, prepayment, paid attraction offers and upsells in the first visit or call.\n- **He raises prices early,** because a price increase lands almost entirely in gross profit and improves every ratio at once.\n- **He uses LTGP:CAC as a health check and payback as the growth limit.** A strong lifetime ratio with slow payback still needs funding.\n- **He computes everything on gross profit and full CAC,** and is conservative with lifetime estimates until there's real data.\n- **Questions he would ask:** What does it cost you to get one customer, including your own time? What's your gross margin? How much gross profit do you collect in the first 30 days? How long until a customer pays back their CAC? What could they buy in the first month that they buy later today? What would happen to your numbers if you raised prices 20%?\n\n### Attraction Offers (link: money/attraction-offers.html)\nTerm: Attraction Offer\nCard: The first offer a new customer sees. Its job is to bring the right people in and, ideally, cover what it cost to get them.\nKey rule: A good attraction offer pulls in the right buyer and pays back part or all of its acquisition cost.\nGist: An attraction offer is the front door of your money model. It is the thing you advertise, the thing a stranger buys first. Its main job is to turn interest into a first transaction with the right kind of customer, at a cost you can afford.\n\n<div class=\"formula\" markdown=\"1\">\n**Attraction offer** = easy first **yes** + filters for the **right buyer** + **recovers acquisition cost** (fully or partly)\n</div>\n\nMost businesses advertise their core product at full price and hope. Alex's view is that the first offer should be designed for getting customers, and the money is made by what follows it. A strong attraction offer is either cheap or free to the buyer in a way that still brings cash in, or it is structured so the buyer pays now and earns value back\nHow Alex would apply it:\n- **He designs the first offer to get customers, and makes money on the back end.** He would accept thin profit at the front if what follows covers the acquisition cost quickly.\n- **He wants the front end to pay for itself where possible.** His rule of thumb is that the attraction offer plus what follows in the first 30 days should cover the cost of acquiring the customer.\n- **He prefers cash now with value back later.** Win Your Money Back and credits toward the next purchase are favourite moves, because they collect money and still feel risk-free.\n- **He ties the conditions to the behaviours that create results.** That way the offer filters out uncommitted buyers and produces success stories.\n- **He tests one attraction offer at a time** and keeps the one that brings in the best buyers at the lowest net cost, not the one with the most sign-ups.\n- **Questions he would ask:** What does it cost you to get one customer today? What is the smallest real result you can sell first? Who do you want to attract, and who do you want to repel? What do buyers purchase next, and how quickly?\n\n### Upsell Offers (link: money/upsell-offers.html)\nTerm: Upsell Offer\nCard: The offer you make right after someone buys. It solves the next problem the first purchase creates, when trust and buying intent are highest.\nKey rule: Every solution creates a new problem: sell the next solution right after the yes.\nGist: An upsell is a second offer made immediately after the first yes. The moment someone buys is the moment they trust you most and are most ready to act. Their wallet is out and their decision is fresh. Waiting a week throws that away.\n\n<div class=\"formula\" markdown=\"1\">\n**Upsell** = the **next problem** the first purchase reveals + offered **right after the yes** + framed as what makes the first purchase work\n</div>\n\nGood upsells are not random extras. Every solution creates a new problem: you bought a training plan, now you need to eat right; you bought a data warehouse, now you need reports on it. The upsell sells the answer to that next problem. Alex describes four ways to do it: the classic, the menu, the anchor and the rollover\nHow Alex would apply it:\n- **He maps the next problem first.** Before designing an upsell, he asks what goes wrong for the customer right after they buy, and sells the fix to that.\n- **He upsells at the moment of the yes.** His default is to make the next offer immediately, while trust and intent are at their peak.\n- **He frames it as completing the purchase.** The upsell is positioned as what makes the first purchase succeed, not as a second sale.\n- **He removes the second payment decision.** Using the card on file or the same invoice is one of his standard moves.\n- **He judges upsells by contribution to 30-day gross profit.** As a rule of thumb, an upsell that many buyers take is often what turns a break-even front end into one that funds growth.\n- **Questions he would ask:** What problem shows up right after they buy? What do your best customers buy next, and when? What do you tell them they don't need? What is your most expensive option, and do you show it first?\n\n### Downsell Offers (link: money/downsell-offers.html)\nTerm: Downsell Offer\nCard: What you offer after someone says no. A smaller, cheaper or easier version that removes the real obstacle, so you keep the customer instead of losing them.\nKey rule: After a no, change the terms, the size or the risk. Do not just cut the price.\nGist: A no is rarely a no to the outcome. More often it is a no to the price, the payment timing, the size of the commitment or the risk. A downsell is a second offer, made right after the no, that removes that specific obstacle.\n\n<div class=\"formula\" markdown=\"1\">\n**Downsell** = find the **real objection** + offer a version that removes it (**payment terms**, **trial**, or **fewer features**) + stop at the **first yes**\n</div>\n\nWithout a downsell, everyone who couldn't or wouldn't take the main offer leaves with nothing, and you lose all the money you spent getting them to the table. With one, many of them become customers, and some of them later move up to the full\nHow Alex would apply it:\n- **He never lets a no end the conversation.** His default is to have a downsell ready for every offer in the sequence.\n- **He diagnoses before he downsells.** He wants to know whether the objection is money, risk or size, and picks the downsell to match.\n- **He changes terms before he changes the product.** Payment plans come first, because the buyer still gets the full offer and you keep full price.\n- **He steps down one rung at a time** and stops at the first yes.\n- **He removes features rather than cutting price.** As a rule of thumb, a lower price should always come with less delivered.\n- **He keeps a path back up.** Credits and pilots give downsold customers a reason to buy the full offer later.\n- **Questions he would ask:** What are the top three reasons people say no? What happens to them today after they say no? What is your payment plan sequence? What could you remove and still deliver a real result?\n\n### Continuity Offers (link: money/continuity-offers.html)\nTerm: Continuity Offer\nCard: Offers that bill the customer again and again for ongoing value. They turn one sale into recurring revenue, and the work is as much about keeping customers as getting them to join.\nKey rule: Get them to join, then get them to stay. Reward commitment, and longer commitments usually churn less.\nGist: A continuity offer is anything the customer pays for on a recurring basis: a membership, a subscription, a retainer, maintenance, ongoing support. It is the last piece of the money model, and often the one that makes the business stable. Each month that a customer stays adds gross profit without another acquisition cost.\n\n<div class=\"formula\" markdown=\"1\">\n**Continuity** = **recurring value** + a reason to **join now** (bonus, discount or waived fee) + a reason to **stay** (commitment and ongoing results)\n</div>\n\nContinuity has two problems to solve. First, getting people to sign up for something that never ends feels like a big commitment. Second, people cancel. Alex's three continuity offers address both: they reward the buyer for joining, and they tie the reward to\nHow Alex would apply it:\n- **He looks for continuity in every business.** His default question is what the customer could keep receiving, because recurring revenue makes acquisition cheaper over time.\n- **He rewards joining and staying.** He stacks a bonus for joining with a discount or waived fee tied to a commitment.\n- **He pushes toward longer terms.** As a rule of thumb, he prefers annual or longer commitments where the market allows, because they bring cash up front and tend to churn less.\n- **He uses upfront cash to fund acquisition.** Prepaid continuity is one of the ways a business can pay for new customers with customers' money.\n- **He keeps the terms clean and visible.** Fees and commitments are explained up front so nobody feels trapped.\n- **Questions he would ask:** What value can you deliver every month? Why do people cancel, and when? What does it cost you to onboard a customer? What would make a customer commit for a year?\n\n### Building Your Money Model (link: money/build-your-money-model.html)\nTerm: Money Model\nCard: How to assemble a money model piece by piece, starting from your core offer, so that each new customer pays for their own acquisition within 30 days.\nKey rule: Core offer first, then attraction, upsell, downsell, continuity. Add one at a time until 30-day gross profit ≥ 2 × CAC.\nGist: A money model is the sequence of offers a customer meets from first contact onwards. You don't design it in one sitting. You start with the thing you already sell well, wrap one offer around it, prove it works, and only then add the next.\n\n<div class=\"formula\" markdown=\"1\">\n**Core offer** → **Attraction** (gets them in, covers CAC) → **Upsell** (at the yes) → **Downsell** (at the no) → **Continuity** (keeps them)\n\n**Target:** 30-day gross profit per customer ≥ **2 × CAC**\n</div>\n\nThe yardstick is cash, measured early. What matters is how much gross profit the average new customer produces in the first 30 days compared with what it cost to get them. When that number beats CAC, growth funds itself. When it beats CAC twice, each customer pays for the next two. The aim is a few products sold through many offers. You change how you package and present the products and leave the products themselves mostly\nHow Alex would apply it:\n- **He starts with the 30-day number.** Before discussing any offer, he wants CAC and 30-day gross profit per customer. Everything else is judged by whether it moves that ratio.\n- **He fixes the biggest gap first.** If the first sale covers little of CAC, he works on the attraction offer. If the first sale is fine but nothing follows, he adds an upsell. He builds in that order and adds one piece at a time.\n- **He puts the upsell where the yes is.** He would move the next offer into the same call, the same checkout or the same readout meeting where the customer just agreed.\n- **He would rather collect cash upfront.** Upfront payments, setup fees and first months bundled into the entry offer pull gross profit into the first 30 days.\n- **He cuts products and adds offers.** When a business has many products and weak economics, he would trim the product list and package the best few in different ways.\n- **He treats the 2× CAC target as the point where growth stops being capped by cash** (a rule of thumb). Below 1×, he would hold back on scaling ads until the model is fixed.\n- **Questions he would ask:** What does a customer cost you, all in? How much gross profit do they bring in the first 30 days? What do people buy right after saying yes? What do the ones who say no actually object to? What keeps a customer paying after the first result? Which one offer would you test next?\n\n## BOOK: Selling & Scaling (From Alex's talks, trainings and podcast)\nLink: sales/\n\n### The CLOSER Framework (link: sales/closer.html)\nTerm: CLOSER Framework\nCard: Six steps that turn a sales call into a diagnosis the buyer walks through themselves. Understand first, show the destination second, clear doubts last.\nKey rule: Clarify, Label, Overview, Sell the vacation, Explain away concerns, Reinforce\nGist: A good sales call is a **diagnosis**. The buyer arrives with a problem, a reason it matters now, and a history of failed attempts to fix it. Your job is to make all three visible to them, then show them where they could be, then help them deal with what still holds them back.\n\n<div class=\"formula\" markdown=\"1\">\n**C**larify why they're here · **L**abel them with a problem · **O**verview past pain · **S**ell the vacation · **E**xplain away concerns · **R**einforce the decision\n</div>\n\nThe order matters more than any single line. The first three letters are entirely about the buyer. Only once they have confirmed the problem in their own words and admitted that past attempts didn't work does it make sense to talk about your offer. A seller who jumps straight to S is pitching to someone who hasn't yet bought the\nHow Alex would apply it:\n- **He treats the call as the buyer's conversation.** The seller asks most of the questions and the buyer does most of the talking, especially in the first half.\n- **He makes the buyer say the problem.** A problem the seller states is an opinion. A problem the buyer confirms is a reason to buy.\n- **He uses past pain as leverage.** What they tried and why it failed becomes the case for doing something different now.\n- **He keeps the pitch short and outcome-first.** If the offer needs a long explanation, he fixes the offer before he fixes the pitch.\n- **He sees most concerns as avoidance of a decision,** and helps the buyer make one, yes or no, instead of leaving it open. As a rule of thumb, a clear no is better than a vague maybe.\n- **He reviews recorded calls against the six steps** to find where deals are lost, then trains that step.\n- **Questions he would ask:** Why did they reach out now? Did they confirm the problem in their own words? What have they already tried, and why did it fail? Did you describe the destination or the process? Which concern did they raise, and did you isolate it? What happened in the first 48 hours after they paid?\n\n### The Constraint (link: sales/constraint.html)\nTerm: Constraint (bottleneck)\nCard: At any moment a business has one main constraint that limits its growth. Work on anything else has little effect until that one is fixed, and then the constraint moves.\nKey rule: Find the one constraint, fix it, find the next one. Test: if leads doubled tomorrow, what breaks first?\nGist: A business works like a pipe with several sections: leads come in, some become sales, sales become delivered work, and delivered clients stay or leave. Only as much flows through the whole pipe as the **narrowest section** lets through. Widening any other section changes nothing, because the flow still waits at the same point.\n\n<div class=\"formula\" markdown=\"1\">\n**One constraint at a time** → fix it → the constraint **moves** → find the next one\n</div>\n\nThe idea comes from Eliyahu Goldratt's **Theory of Constraints**, set out in his book *The Goal*. Alex uses it as a simple operating rule for founders: find the one thing that limits growth right now, put your effort there, and say no to good ideas elsewhere until the constraint has\nHow Alex would apply it:\n- **He asks what the constraint is before giving any advice.** Most business questions have a different right answer depending on where the bottleneck sits.\n- **He runs the doubling test first,** then checks it against the funnel numbers.\n- **He puts the owner's time on the constraint** and cuts or pauses projects that don't touch it, even promising ones.\n- **He is wary of adding leads to a broken funnel.** Fixing conversion, delivery or retention first makes every future lead worth more.\n- **He exploits before he elevates:** squeeze more out of the current team, process and offer before hiring or spending.\n- **He expects the constraint to move** and reviews it regularly, treating a new bottleneck as a sign of progress.\n- **Questions he would ask:** If leads doubled tomorrow, what breaks first? What are your numbers at each step? Where do people or work queue up? How much of delivery still depends on you? What would you stop doing this month if you took the answer seriously?\n\n## BOOK: Alex's Playbook (How Alex diagnoses and decides, distilled from 100+ videos)\nLink: playbook/\n\nThe four books describe Alex's frameworks: how to build an offer, get leads, structure a money model and close. This layer captures something different: how he thinks when a real owner tells him about a real problem. It is distilled from 103 of his videos (hotseats, live Q&As, lectures and sales trainings). The transcripts stay private. What you read here is paraphrased, organised by theme, and every entry lists the videos it comes from, with timestamps.\n\nThe books answer \"what is the framework?\". The playbook answers \"what would he do first, what would he ignore, and why?\". It favours patterns that recur across many videos, marks single-video points as such, and keeps his numbers as rules of thumb with their context. Where the notes say nothing, the playbook says nothing.\n\nAcross all of the videos, the same operating principles keep coming back:\n\n1. **Find the one constraint.** First triage is supply versus demand: if customers doubled, what would break? Then attack the funnel stage furthest below benchmark and ignore the rest.\n2. **The stated problem is rarely the real one.** \"Leads\" often turns out to be the offer or the sales process, \"ads\" turns out to be LTV, \"people\" turns out to be a broken model.\n3. **Numbers before advice.** He asks in a fixed order: what you sell, to whom, at what price, how you acquire, close rate, LTV, CAC, churn, gross margin, capacity. Lifetime gross profit to CAC is the core lens.\n4. **More, then better, then new.** Squeeze what already works before trying anything new. Never switch off a working channel to test another.\n5. **Focus and subtract.** One business, one avatar, one channel, one priority, roughly until $1M a year. Kill side ventures and losing units.\n6. **Price is the first and cheapest lever.** Most owners are underpriced; very high close rates and thin margins are warning signs. Add bonuses instead of discounting.\n7. **Pull cash forward.** Solve cash problems through offer design (prepay, up-front fees, quick upsells) before debt or capital, so CAC comes back within about 30 days.\n8. **Fix the leaky bucket before buying traffic.** Retention and lifetime value are where the advantage lives, and churn is usually an onboarding or communication problem.\n9. **Talent is the long-term constraint.** Recruit like you acquire customers, pay more for people who have done it before, and judge people by the metrics they track.\n10. **Move the founder from doer to teacher to leader.** Reducing founder dependence is the route from income to enterprise value.\n11. **Nail it before you scale it.** No second location, product or business until the first is profitable and runs without the owner. Better before bigger.\n12. **Change is costly, so choose few moves.** Two or three highest-impact moves, sequenced by impact, confidence and ease, one change at a time.\n\n### The Diagnostic Sequence (link: playbook/diagnostic-sequence.html)\nTerm: Diagnostic Sequence\nCard: The fixed order of questions Alex runs on any business before he gives advice. Numbers first, then the one constraint, then two or three moves.\nKey rule: Numbers → supply or demand → weakest stage vs benchmark → one \"how do I…\" question → 2–3 moves\nGist: Alex diagnoses almost every business the same way. He asks for the numbers before he listens to the story, because the problem the owner brings is rarely the problem the numbers show. \"We need more leads\" turns out to be an offer problem, \"ads stopped working\" turns out to be a lifetime value problem, and \"my team is the issue\" turns out to be a broken model.\n\nThen he sorts the business into one of two buckets: it cannot get enough customers (demand) or it cannot serve more customers (supply). Inside that bucket he finds the single stage that sits furthest below a healthy benchmark, names it as a \"how do I…\" question, and limits his advice to the two or three moves that attack it.\n\n<div class=\"formula\" markdown=\"1\">\n**Numbers** → **Supply or demand?** → **Weakest stage vs benchmark** → **One \"how do I…\" question** → **2–3 moves**, then the owner's real goal decides the\nHow Alex would apply it:\n- **Numbers before opinions.** He asks for price, volume, close rate, churn, lifetime value, acquisition cost and gross margin before he says anything about strategy.\n- **Supply or demand first.** If doubling customers would break delivery, he works on capacity and price. If not, it is a pure demand problem, and he works on offer and traffic.\n- **The weakest stage gets everything.** He benchmarks each funnel stage and puts all testing on the one furthest below its norm.\n- **One constraint, phrased as a skill.** He turns it into a \"how do I…\" question and refuses side topics until it is solved.\n- **Short ordered list.** Two or three moves, cheapest and highest impact first, working parts left alone.\n- **A new venture is a red flag.** If the owner would drop the new idea once the current business tripled, it is an escape from the constraint.\n- **Questions he would ask:** What do you sell, to whom, at what price? Where do customers come from? What are your show and close rates? What is a customer worth and what does one cost? If customers doubled, what breaks? Why aren't you spending ten times more on ads? What do you want: double it, sell it, or take cash out?\n\n### Business Math & Benchmarks (link: playbook/business-math.html)\nTerm: Business Math (LTGP, LTV:CAC, payback)\nCard: The handful of numbers Alex runs in his head on every business, and the benchmarks he compares them to. Choosing between options becomes a math problem.\nKey rule: LTGP = price ÷ churn × gross margin; 30-day cash > CAC + delivery cost\nGist: Alex treats business math as a language: you can only think as clearly about a business as your vocabulary for its numbers allows. In conversation he runs the same small set of calculations before he advises anyone: what a customer is worth in gross profit, what it costs to get one, how fast that cost comes back, and what the goal implies in leads, calls and budget.\n\nThere are only two ways to grow: more customers, or more value per customer. He keeps finding that the second is easier. Since acquisition cost for a given customer is roughly the same for every competitor in a market, the lasting advantage goes to whoever makes a customer worth the most.\n\n<div class=\"formula\" markdown=\"1\">\n**LTGP** = (price ÷ monthly churn) × gross margin  ·  **Max CAC** = LTGP  ·  **Client-financed** when 30-day cash collected > CAC + cost to\nHow Alex would apply it:\n- **He calculates in the conversation.** Churn, LTV, LTGP, cap and payback are worked out on the spot, and he expects the owner to know them without a finance report.\n- **Value per customer before more customers.** Price, churn and upsells usually move profit more, and with less effort, than doubling acquisition.\n- **Gross margin is the first health check.** A service under 80% gets fixed through price or delivery before anything is scaled.\n- **Cash forward until CAC pays back in 30 days.** Setup fees, prepay and early upsells come before debt or investors.\n- **Decisions by math.** Pricing and offer choices are settled by conversion × price × LTV per option, never by opinion.\n- **Spend while the math holds.** If a dollar reliably returns several, the budget is \"as much as possible\", judged on absolute profit.\n- **Questions he would ask:** What do you charge, and what does it cost to deliver? How many cancel each month? What is a customer worth in gross profit? What does one cost to acquire, and how fast do you get it back? What is your close rate against your price? How many leads a day does your goal need?\n\n### Focus & Subtraction (link: playbook/focus-and-subtraction.html)\nTerm: Focus, 1-1-1, subtraction\nCard: Alex's default fix for a stuck or overwhelmed owner is to take things away. One business, one avatar, one channel and one priority, held long enough to compound.\nKey rule: 1-1-1 = one product, one avatar, one channel until $1M a year. Subtract before you add.\nGist: Across the hotseats, the most common thing Alex prescribes is removal. Owners arrive with two businesses, three offers, five channels, a side software product or a partner deal on the side, and describe the result as overwhelm or a plateau. He reads both as self-made complexity. Focus, in his usage, is something you get by subtracting, and he judges it by the number and quality of things you say no to.\n\n<div class=\"formula\" markdown=\"1\">\n**1-1-1:** one product, one avatar, one channel until about **$1M a year**. Then: **one priority** at a time, and **subtract before you add**.\n</div>\n\nThe logic is economic. An owner's attention is the scarcest resource in a small company, and he treats strategy as the allocation of limited time, money and people across unlimited options. Every split of attention and every change has a cost, usually higher than the owner\nHow Alex would apply it:\n- **He asks which unit actually makes money**, and where sales really happen, then points everything there and closes the other doors.\n- **He makes the owner list subtractions before any addition** and checks whether the freed hours are enough for the new thing.\n- **He names the red dress out loud** when a new venture, avatar or whale shows up at a plateau, and redirects to the constraint in the current business.\n- **He treats change as expensive**: one real change at a time, a risk-adjusted 20% bar, real-time metrics to see the dip, and a queue for every other idea.\n- **He picks one priority for the year** and protects it, letting small fires burn rather than starting parallel fixes after 30 days.\n- **He simplifies the product and the message**: fewer deliverables, one core promise, the shortest script that works.\n- **Questions he would ask:** Which line makes the money? If a friend had one business everyone wanted plus four slower side ventures, what would you tell them? If you only did this one thing, would you hit your goal? What can we delete and still have it be as good or better? Would you still want this new venture if the current business tripled?\n\n### Prioritising Moves (link: playbook/prioritising-moves.html)\nTerm: More, Better, New; ICE (impact, confidence, ease)\nCard: How Alex orders the work once he knows the constraint. Squeeze what already works, rank everything else by impact, confidence and ease, and give the owner only two or three moves.\nKey rule: More → Better → New, ranked by Impact × Confidence × Ease\nGist: Most hotseats end the same way. Alex writes a short numbered list, with quick, likely, high-impact moves at the top and heavy strategic bets at the bottom. Before anything new appears on that list, he asks why the owner cannot simply do more of what already works. New ideas have to prove they beat spending more on the existing winner.\n\n<div class=\"formula\" markdown=\"1\">\n**More** of what works → make it **better** → only then something **new**.\nRank candidates by **Impact × Confidence × Ease**, and hand over **two or three**.\n</div>\n\nThe ordering is how he controls risk. A move that adds volume to a proven channel has known economics. A move that changes the offer, the avatar or the channel resets the learning curve. He picks up the obvious money first and lets the cash and confidence from those wins fund the harder bets\nHow Alex would apply it:\n- **He asks \"why can't you just do more of this?\"** before discussing anything new, and treats the answer as the next constraint.\n- **He writes a numbered list in ICE order**: quick, likely, high-impact items first, structural bets last, dependencies respected.\n- **He fixes offer and price first**, then conversion, then volume on proven channels, and only then new channels.\n- **He keeps every working channel running** while testing, and isolates new variables (one closer, one source, one change).\n- **He leaves alone what customers love** and what the metrics say is healthy.\n- **He cuts the plan to two or three moves** the owner will actually carry out.\n- **Questions he would ask:** Where do your best customers come from, and have you scaled that? If you could do only one thing this year and it had to double the business, what would it be? What happened when you increased spend? What breaks if we do more? Is this new idea better than simply spending more on what works?\n\n### Risk & Bets (link: playbook/risk-and-bets.html)\nTerm: Risk-adjusted bets; push vs pivot\nCard: Alex prices every move as payoff times probability, prefers the surer bet, keeps pushing while the core assumptions hold, and treats fear-driven under-spending, debt and premature hiring as the risks that actually hurt owners.\nKey rule: Bet value = payoff × probability. At equal value, take the higher probability.\nGist: Alex talks about risk in two directions at once. He thinks owners overestimate the downside of the right moves (raising price, spending more on a channel that pays, hiring salespeople when leads exist) and underestimate the downside of the wrong ones (starting a second business, taking on debt, adding staff ahead of growth, changing course on a bad week).\n\n<div class=\"formula\" markdown=\"1\">\n**Bet value** = **payoff** × **probability**\nAt equal value, choose the **higher probability**, then string sure wins together.\n**Push** while the first-principles assumptions hold. **Pivot** only when data disproves one.\n</div>\n\nHe frames risk as personal and usually overestimated: for most founders the real downside is bankruptcy and starting over. The scarce resource is time, since a founder only gets a handful of multi-year attempts in a\nHow Alex would apply it:\n- **He asks for the payoff and the odds** and prefers the surer path when the products are close.\n- **He chooses execution risk over idea risk** and wants proven demand before any new venture.\n- **He pushes while the written assumptions hold** and pivots only on data, not on fatigue.\n- **He runs the escape test** before discussing any new business: would you still want it if the current one tripled?\n- **He removes fear-based caps** on spend and sales hiring when unit economics and cash flow support it, and frames testing as an investment with a budget.\n- **He keeps fixed costs and debt low**, pays down debt, takes profit out, and does not hire ahead of growth (commission reps excepted).\n- **Questions he would ask:** Has a fundamental assumption about demand been disproven? How much are you spending, can you spend more, and why don't you? If your return were 10:1 instead of 3:1, would you spend more? What is your monthly break-even and debt service? Would you still want this if the current business tripled?\n\n### Pricing Decisions (link: playbook/pricing-decisions.html)\nTerm: Pricing (raise, test, never discount the core)\nCard: Price is the lever Alex reaches for first: it costs nothing to change, falls straight to profit, and most owners are underpriced. He tests it by conversion × price and almost never lowers it.\nKey rule: Raise price until conversion × price (or total gross profit) starts to fall, not until unit sales fall.\nGist: Across his hotseats, Alex treats price as the first and cheapest lever. Changing it means changing a number or a sentence, with almost no operational drag, yet owners fear it more than anything else. His recurring diagnosis is that small owners price from their own wallet: they cannot imagine anyone paying more than they would, and they discount whatever comes easily to them.\n\nThe math is why he cares. On thin margins, a small price rise multiplies profit, while more customers only add to it. In one keynote he walks through a business with a 20% margin: doubling the number of customers doubles profit, but doubling the price makes profit six times larger, because the extra revenue carries no extra cost.\n\n<div class=\"formula\" markdown=\"1\">\n**Test price by** close rate × price (revenue per sales conversation), or total gross profit. **Stop raising** when that number falls, not when the first buyer says\nHow Alex would apply it:\n- **He checks price before demand.** Thin margin, full calendar, very high close rate, or unaffordable acquisition all point him to price first, ahead of new channels or hires.\n- **He runs the numbers in the room.** Margin, close rate and units turn the decision into arithmetic: a 20% cut at a 40% margin needs twice the sales to break even.\n- **He raises, then backs it with value.** If the owner can't justify a higher price, he improves the offer (faster, easier, more certain) and adds a guarantee with teeth, then raises 20–40%.\n- **He refuses to discount the core.** Terms, components and bonuses can move; the core price does not. Any discount is traded for something.\n- **He builds a ladder of tiers.** Premium first, a 10x anchor above, a downsell below, priced so the core looks best.\n- **He accepts losing the worst customers.** Watch what customers do, not what they say: if they keep buying while complaining, ignore the complaints.\n- **Questions he would ask:** How do you set prices today, and who set them? What is your close rate at this price? When did you last raise prices? What is your gross margin? Are you full? Which segment is least price-sensitive? What revenue per call do you get at price A versus price B?\n\n### Pull Cash Forward (link: playbook/cash-forward.html)\nTerm: Pull cash forward (30-day payback)\nCard: Alex treats up-front cash as the master variable. If the first 30 days of a customer pay for acquiring and serving them, growth stops being limited by money, so he fixes cash flow through offer design before anyone talks about debt or investors.\nKey rule: Cash collected in the first 30 days > cost of delivery + cost of acquiring the customer (CAC).\nGist: In the notes, cash flow problems are almost never solved with a loan or a funding round. Alex solves them by changing how and when the customer pays: prepay discounts, setup fees, quarterly or annual billing, layaway, card on file, a planned ladder of payment options. The pattern recurs across hotseats, lectures and sales trainings, and up-front cash works as the master variable behind much of his advice.\n\nThe reasoning is simple. If a new customer returns more cash in the first month than they cost to acquire and serve, every customer funds the next one. He ties the 30-day window to the interest-free period on a credit card: it is about as long as a small business can carry the cost of acquisition without its own money.\n\n<div class=\"formula\" markdown=\"1\">\n**30-day rule:** cash collected in the first 30 days > fully loaded delivery cost + CAC.\n**Stronger version (one keynote):** 30-day gross profit from one customer > cost to acquire and serve two\nHow Alex would apply it:\n- **He asks for the 30-day number first.** What is a customer worth in gross profit within 30 days, and what does it cost to acquire and serve them?\n- **He redesigns the offer before the financing.** Setup fees, prepay, annual default, layaway. Debt and capital come after the offer cannot be improved.\n- **He anchors high and steps down.** Payment-plan price first, prepay discount second, then financing, partial prepay, plan, smaller commitment. Everyone buys something.\n- **He uses perks before percentages.** Faster, safer, easier, or a bonus worth more to the buyer than it costs you.\n- **He goes to existing customers when cash is urgent.** Prepay for extra service, credits toward bigger packages, one strong offer to the list.\n- **He removes the money ceiling, then spends.** Once acquisition funds itself, the limit should be operational, not financial.\n- **Questions he would ask:** How much cash do you collect in the first 30 days? What is your CAC, and how long until it's paid back? Do you offer prepay or financing, and what share takes it? Do you have a card on file? How much cash do you have, and can you deliver what you've already sold with it?\n\n### Retention & Lifetime Value (link: playbook/retention-and-ltv.html)\nTerm: Retention, LTV and LTGP\nCard: If Alex could give a business one advantage, it would be revenue retention. He fixes churn before scaling acquisition, and he reads churn as an onboarding, usage or expectation problem far more often than a sign the product is bad.\nKey rule: LTV = price ÷ monthly churn. LTGP = LTV × gross margin. Revenue plateaus at new sales per month × LTV.\nGist: In several lectures Alex calls retention the most valuable property a business can have. Retained customers stack year on year and growth compounds. Without retention you are permanently in the business of replacing customers, and a business that depends only on new sales dies when its channel breaks. Since the cost of reaching a given customer is roughly the same for every competitor, the lasting edge is the lifetime value you build on top.\n\nHis order is consistent: plug the leaks, then pour in more. Scaling acquisition into a leaking bucket just scales the problems. And when he digs into churn, the cause is usually upstream of the product: weak onboarding, customers who don't use what they bought, or a sales promise that didn't match reality.\n\n<div class=\"formula\" markdown=\"1\">\n**Churn** = cancels this month ÷ active clients · **LTV** = price ÷ churn · **LTGP** (lifetime gross profit) = LTV × gross margin\n**Ceiling:** new sales per month × LTV is roughly where revenue stops\nHow Alex would apply it:\n- **He calculates churn and LTV first.** Most owners don't know either. He works out the plateau before talking about growth.\n- **He fixes retention before acquisition,** unless retention is already strong, in which case he sends the effort to acquisition.\n- **He starts with onboarding.** If there is none, it is the first fix; then unlockables, exit interviews and usage tracking, one tactic at a time.\n- **He defines activation from the best customers** and points the first 30 days at it.\n- **He makes annual the default** and separates one-time value from the recurring fee.\n- **He places the referral ask and the upsell at the moment of need,** right after activation and right after purchase.\n- **Questions he would ask:** How many customers cancel each month, out of how many active? What is your price and gross margin? How many of last year's customers are still paying, and how much of last year's revenue? What does onboarding look like? What is your activation point? Why did the last 20 leave, and why do your longest customers stay? Where exactly does the referral ask happen?\n\n### The Sales Machine (link: playbook/sales-machine.html)\nTerm: Sales team system (speed to lead, feed the killers, drill and review)\nCard: How Alex builds selling into a system: what surrounds the call, who gets which lead, a question-led call, a planned answer to every objection, and a team trained by daily drills and recorded calls.\nKey rule: Availability × speed × show rate × close rate × cash collected. Fix around the call before you fix the call.\nGist: Alex treats sales as a machine with many small levers, each raising the odds that someone buys. His most repeated claim across the sales trainings is that what happens *around* the call (availability, speed to lead, booking, reminders, lead routing) usually moves revenue more than what is said *on* it. He fixes those first because they are mostly one-time setup work.\n\n<div class=\"formula\" markdown=\"1\">\n**Sales** = opportunities (availability, speed, show rate) × conversion (diagnosis, short pitch, objections) × consistency (training, review, standards)\n</div>\n\nThe call itself follows the [CLOSER framework](../sales/closer.html), which this entry does not repeat. Here the focus is the system around it: lead routing, rules for questions and objections, the handoff into delivery, and how he trains, ranks, pays and recruits\nHow Alex would apply it:\n- **He fixes around the call first.** Speed to lead, same-day slots and reminders come before script changes.\n- **He treats show rate and close rate as equal.** A 20% lift in either is worth the same, so he picks whichever is easier from where you are.\n- **He routes leads deliberately:** best to the killers, worst to the trainees, no group complaints.\n- **He never cuts price to close.** Terms or components change, never the price, and price tests are planned in advance.\n- **He coaches one fix at a time** and judges reps on sub-metrics, not close rate alone.\n- **He makes the founder sell until about $1M,** then checks the model still works when reps close at half his rate.\n- **Questions he would ask:** How fast do you call new leads? What percentage book, show, get an offer and close? How booked are the calendars? Is the team following the script word for word? Which one fix would move this rep's close rate most? Would one more rep earn more than they cost?\n\n### Hiring & Talent (link: playbook/hiring-and-talent.html)\nTerm: Who, not how\nCard: Past the first stage, the speed of a company is the speed at which it recruits. Alex treats talent as the long-term constraint and runs hiring with the same funnel, metrics and standards as customer acquisition.\nKey rule: Who before how. Hire A-players for the smallest skill gap, pay them well, interview far more than feels reasonable.\nGist: In almost every lecture on scaling, Alex ends up at the same place: once demand is solved, the business grows only as fast as it can find, train and keep good people. When an owner lists six problems, he often reads them as one: the company is missing around three senior hires. Above roughly $30M a year he describes his own bottleneck as nothing but talent.\n\n<div class=\"formula\" markdown=\"1\">\n**Scaling speed** = **speed of recruiting talent**. Ask \"who\" before \"how\": often the fastest answer is someone who has already done it.\n</div>\n\nHe backs this with blunt economics. In his framing an A-player costs a bit more and produces several times as much, so paying properly for the right person is one of the largest arbitrages in business. His recurring rules of thumb: hire when the freed time or capacity returns several times the salary (he mentions 4 to 5x), and treat each new salary band as a new territory that reveals talent you did not know\nHow Alex would apply it:\n- **He checks whether talent is really the constraint.** If the model has no margin, he fixes price or delivery before any hire.\n- **He makes recruiting the owner's job when it is the bottleneck,** compressing it hard: many interviews in one week, tracked like a sales pipeline.\n- **He buys experience for senior roles** and accepts a short-term profit dip to pay for higher-level leaders, framing it as skipping years of mistakes.\n- **He interviews with the two tests** (metrics, how the role makes money) and passes on leaders he cannot learn from.\n- **He sets standards visibly and enforces them on everyone,** including the top performer, with time-boxed performance plans.\n- **He refuses** sideways moves for failing people, inflated titles in small teams, and pay raises that do not solve a recruiting or retention problem.\n- **Questions he would ask:** If you had ten of your best person, would you make more money? What are you doing this week to recruit? How many candidates per hire, how fast is first contact? What metrics does this person use for their job? How does their role make money? Does this hire raise the team's average?\n\n### Delegation & the Owner's Role (link: playbook/delegation-and-owner.html)\nTerm: Buy back your time\nCard: Alex sees the founder as the default bottleneck. He buys back time in a fixed order and delegates through a written system, until the business keeps growing while the owner is away.\nKey rule: Time study → task list → process → decision rules with limits → KPIs → hand off at ~80% as good.\nGist: Early on, a business is a suit of armour that multiplies the owner but cannot move without them. Alex's goal is a business that creates value without you: only then is it worth something to someone else, and only then can you step away. Two companies with the same profit are worth very different amounts if one needs its owner eighty hours a week.\n\n<div class=\"formula\" markdown=\"1\">\n**Delegation** = time study → task list → **process** → decision rules with limits → **KPIs** → graduation test (about 80% as good) → shadow, supervise, support\n</div>\n\nHe treats this as a sequence of giving up control. At each stage, the thing you hold on to is the next thing you must hand off, and it always feels uncomfortable. Handing over control never hands over accountability: you stay responsible for the result and close enough to know where the problems\nHow Alex would apply it:\n- **He starts with a time study,** never with a guess. The log shows where the hours go.\n- **He hands off from the bottom of the value list** and keeps going in the fixed order: support, admin, selling, the sales team, leaders.\n- **He wants decisions on paper,** with if-then rules and a spending limit, so people can act without calling the owner.\n- **He sets a graduation bar** at roughly 80% of the owner's quality and accepts less at first if improvement is visible.\n- **He diagnoses before he blames:** expectation, then training, then motivation.\n- **He tests independence for real** by having the owner leave for a month, then longer, and fixing what breaks.\n- **Questions he would ask:** What did your last week look like in 15-minute blocks? Which tasks could someone do for a fraction of your hourly value? Can you name each department's current problems? Would the sales model still work if reps closed half as well as you? Did they know it was expected, do they know how, and do they know why? What are you holding on to because you enjoy it?\n\n### Stages of Growth (link: playbook/stages-of-growth.html)\nTerm: Stages of growth (scaling roadmap)\nCard: Each revenue stage has its own constraint and asks the founder to give up the job that got them there. Nail the current unit before you multiply it.\nKey rule: Nail it before you scale it: no second location, product or business until the first is profitable and runs without you.\nGist: Alex treats growth as a series of levels, like a video game. Each one has a different boss, and the skills that beat the last boss don't beat the next one. That is why he places almost every hotseat business on his head-count-based scaling roadmap before giving advice: the right move at 5 people is often the wrong move at 50.\n\nTwo rules run through all of it. First, at each stage the thing you are holding on to is the thing you have to give up next: delivery, then selling, then marketing, then leading. Second, better before bigger. He refuses to help a business multiply a unit that doesn't work yet, because you only scale the problem.\n\n<div class=\"formula\" markdown=\"1\">\n**Nail it** (profitable unit, runs without you) → **squeeze it** (more of what works) → **only then multiply it** (new location, product,\nHow Alex would apply it:\n- **He places the business first.** Headcount, revenue and who does what decide which stage's rules apply.\n- **He names what the founder must give up next,** and frames it as the next level rather than a loss of control. Accountability stays with the founder.\n- **He blocks expansion until the unit is nailed:** profitable, payback known, and growing for months without the owner.\n- **He squeezes existing capacity first:** revenue per location, price, close rate, then new units.\n- **He accepts a short-term profit hit to hire leaders** when the goal is beyond the founder's own hours, and treats each higher salary band as new territory.\n- **He pauses scaling around $1–5M to fix experience** when CAC rises faster than ad costs, a sign of negative word of mouth.\n- **Questions he would ask:** How many people work here? Does the core unit make money at full capacity? Does it run without you for three months? What are you holding on to? If you doubled customers, what would break? What do you actually want: many locations, one big one, or an exit?\n\n### Enterprise Value (link: playbook/enterprise-value.html)\nTerm: Enterprise value\nCard: The difference between owning a well-paid job and owning an asset. A business is worth what it earns without you, multiplied by how little risk a buyer takes on.\nKey rule: Enterprise value = (customers × lifetime gross profit) ÷ risk\nGist: Alex separates income from wealth. Income comes from work. Wealth comes from owning things that create value without you. If a business shrinks materially when the owner steps away, he calls it a high-paying job you can't quit, not an asset. Two companies with the same profit can be worth wildly different amounts: one needs the owner 80 hours a week and cannot be sold, the other runs without them and sells at a multiple.\n\nHe frames value as a fraction. The top is how many customers you have and how much gross profit each brings over their lifetime. The bottom is risk: everything that makes a buyer worry the profit won't survive the sale.\n\n<div class=\"formula\" markdown=\"1\">\n**Enterprise value** = (**customers** × **lifetime gross profit**) ÷ **risk**\n</div>\n\nMost of his enterprise-value advice is about shrinking the denominator: concentration risk, churn and founder\nHow Alex would apply it:\n- **He asks the goal first:** sell eventually, or just make money? If only money, he keeps it simple and runs the working playbook more times.\n- **He audits concentration across all four risks** and fixes the one an acquirer would discount most.\n- **He removes the founder from acquisition before delivery,** because founder-driven lead flow is the biggest key-person risk.\n- **He prioritises retention over new revenue** when the goal is enterprise value, aiming for roughly 80% annual retention.\n- **He prices hard problems in enterprise value terms:** losing $100k over six months to find a second channel is cheap if it adds $10–15M of value to a $2M-EBITDA business at 5x.\n- **He invests surplus cash in talent and brand** in service businesses, and refuses to trade long-term brand for a short-term sale.\n- **Questions he would ask:** Is anything in this business dependent on you? What would an acquirer see as its weaknesses? What share of revenue comes from your top client, channel and supplier? What is annual retention? Who buys businesses like this, and at what multiple? Which part is the goose, and what are the eggs?\n\n### Cases: Local Businesses (link: playbook/cases-local.html)\nTerm: Hotseat cases\nCard: Forty-two real situations from gyms, clinics, dental and chiropractic practices, salons, restaurants and home-service contractors, with what Alex diagnosed and what he told the owner to do.\nKey rule: Local businesses are usually underpriced and under-advertised, and their ceiling is the owner or one expert doing all the work.\nGist: Local owners almost always arrive saying they need more leads. When Alex looks at the numbers, the leads are rarely the first problem. Close rates of 70 to 90 percent, show rates near 100 percent and ads returning 10:1 or better tell him the business is underpriced and spending far too little on a channel that already works. His first moves are usually a price increase of 10 to 40 percent, a dedicated landing page and more ad spend on the channel that is already profitable. Small changes per ticket (menu order, an attached drink, a rebooking step) go straight to profit in a high-volume business.\n\nThe second pattern is capacity. Many local businesses are capped by one doctor, one founder in the van or a shortage of technicians. Alex treats recruiting as a core business function with its own funnel and budget, and he works out what one practitioner or rep is worth in gross profit to show the owner how cheap a large hiring bonus really is. He moves the expert to the hardest work and gives the simpler half to associates.\n\nThe third pattern is cash timing. Quarterly billing, pay-per-visit models and fronted equipment starve growing local businesses. He restructures the offer so more cash comes in on day one: a front-end assessment with the card on file, a prepaid year, an enrolment fee, a first quarter paid up front. Underneath all of it sits focus. He cuts extra avatars, side ...\nHow Alex would apply it:\n**First questions.**\n1. What are your show rate, close rate and return on ad spend? Very high numbers mean you are underpriced and underspending.\n2. Are you limited by demand (empty chairs, open slots) or by supply (waiting list, owner or expert fully booked)?\n3. How much cash do you collect on day one per customer, and does it cover the cost of getting and serving them?\n4. How many avatars, services, locations and side ventures are you running, and which one makes the money?\n5. What does one more technician, practitioner or rep bring in gross profit per year?\n\n**Default moves by situation.**\n- **High close rate, open capacity:** raise price 10–20%, build a dedicated landing page, then increase spend on the channel that already works until returns drop toward about 5:1.\n- **Waiting list or expert at capacity:** move simple work to associates, make recruiting a funnel with a budget sized to a hire's gross profit, then raise price.\n- **Cash squeeze:** restructure the offer so more cash comes up front (assessment with card on file, prepaid year, enrolment fee, first quarter up front), cut the lowest-margin payers or services, and stop new initiatives.\n- **Lots of leads, poor conversion:** call leads within a minute, sell the same day after the assessment, add a VIP tier and name the differentiators.\n- **One-time sale:** add a membership or maintenance plan, rebook before the customer leaves, and ask for a referral at each visit.\n- **Owner wants a new venture or national expansion:** first dominate the current market and fix the current constraint; a new business rarely fixes the skill gap behind the plateau.\n- **Multiple locations:** fix profit and payback per location before opening the next, and bring in a finance lead and a seasoned operator.\nPatterns across cases:\n- **Underpriced, then a price rise first (about 10 cases).** Close rates of 70–90% and full calendars read to him as underpricing. Typical moves: 10–40% increases, VIP tiers, weekend or same-day surcharges, priority fees. He watches the close rate and holds the price while it stays healthy.\n- **Underspending on a channel that already works (about 8 cases).** Search or social returning 5:1 to 13:1 on a tiny budget, an untapped door-to-door team or networking. He scales the winner before testing anything new, and judges success by total profit.\n- **Capacity capped by one expert or too few staff (about 9 cases).** Founder-doctors, owners in the van, missing technicians, cleaners and practitioners. He prices out what one hire is worth in gross profit and makes recruiting a funnel with a real budget.\n- **Cash collected too late (about 7 cases).** Quarterly billing, pay-per-visit, slow resident profit. He moves cash forward with card-on-file assessments, prepaid years, enrolment fees and up-front bundles.\n- **Too many avatars, channels or ventures (about 8 cases).** Trash with two avatars and five channels, clinics with many services, owners with side ventures. He cuts to one avatar, one channel and one model before expanding.\n- **Weak front door and slow selling (about 6 cases).** No dedicated landing page, sales delayed to day two, leads called back late. He adds a cheap front-end offer, same-day selling and an instant call-back.\n- **Missing back end and rebooking (about 6 cases).** No membership, no rebook, no maintenance plan, no second visit for first-timers. He adds a default membership or plan and an ask at every visit.\n- **Owner, partner or leadership gap (about 8 cases).** Misaligned partners, missing finance function, no senior operators. He fixes partnerships first and pays up for experienced leaders, accepting lower short-term profit.\n- **Leave a loved product alone (2–3 cases, from his own gyms).** When customers love it, replicate or raise price; spend where customers see value.\n\n### Cases: Service & B2B Businesses (link: playbook/cases-services.html)\nTerm: Hotseat cases\nCard: Thirty-six real situations from agencies, consultancies, law and accounting firms, staffing, B2B services and real-estate and insurance businesses, with what Alex diagnosed and what he told the owner to do.\nKey rule: Service businesses are usually underpriced, sold by the hour, fed by one channel the owner doesn't control, and leaking clients in the first months.\nGist: Service owners usually come to Alex with a growth goal and a vague sense that they need more leads. In most of these cases the business is underpriced, priced by the hour or by cost-plus, and sold to anyone who asks. His first move is to repackage the work as an outcome with a clear price, often a fixed fee with a performance component, an anchor tier far above it and a cheaper downsell below. He reads close rates near 100% as proof the price is too low, and he expects some fall in close rate when the price rises.\n\nThe second pattern is acquisition that depends on something the owner doesn't control: vendor referrals, a handful of partners, the founder's network, one ad platform. He adds an owned channel, usually outbound to a defined list (top agents by volume, high-spending advertisers, partner types), and keeps doing more of whatever already works before trying something new. Partnerships and affiliates get real attention in his advice; he wants them activated, measured per partner and given an offer that changes their behaviour.\n\nThe third pattern is churn. Agencies and recurring services lose clients in months three to six, and he traces it to the wrong clients and a weak start more than to the work itself. He tightens the avatar to those who activate, adds qualification friction before the sale, makes the first 30 days deliver a visible win and keeps communicating what ...\nHow Alex would apply it:\n**First questions.**\n1. How do you price: hourly, cost-plus, or by outcome? What is your close rate?\n2. Where does each new client come from, and how much of it do you control?\n3. How many clients reach month six, and what do the ones who stay have in common?\n4. What happens in the first 30 days, and when does the client see a visible win?\n5. If you sold tomorrow, what would a buyer actually be paying for?\n\n**Default moves by situation.**\n- **Close rate very high, hourly billing:** repackage as an outcome with a fixed fee, add a performance component, an anchor tier above and a downsell below; accept a lower close rate.\n- **All work from referrals or one partner:** build outbound to a defined list (top performers in the target market, big spenders), and run a partner programme with an offer partners care about.\n- **Churn in months three to six:** define the avatar from your best long-stay clients, qualify on the form and the call, set expectations up front, make onboarding personal and show the results regularly.\n- **Selling high-ticket to cold traffic:** add a cheaper front-end step (audit, assessment, pay per show) and roll its value into the main offer.\n- **Too many offers or units:** keep the one with demand and margin, turn the others into marketing assets or close them.\n- **Delivery capacity full:** raise price first, add offshore staff and AI to the workflow, and keep selling.\n- **Planning an exit:** add a second channel, remove the founder from the front, raise retention, or roll up the clients you serve.\nPatterns across cases:\n- **Underpriced, hourly or cost-plus (about 9 cases).** Hourly legal work, invented line items, capped subcontractor rates, close rates of 84–100%. He moves to an outcome price, often a fixed fee plus a performance share, with a high anchor and a cheaper downsell.\n- **Acquisition depends on something the owner doesn't control (about 9 cases).** Vendor referrals, a few partners, the founder's network, one ad platform. He adds an owned channel, usually outbound to a defined list, and keeps scaling what already works.\n- **Partners and affiliates under-used (about 6 cases).** Referral partners bringing a third of revenue from a few emails, community partners, partner-level retention. He scales partner outreach, gives partners an offer that changes behaviour, and measures value per partner.\n- **Churn from the wrong clients and a weak start (about 7 cases).** Agencies losing clients in months three to six, CPA clients leaving at 70% retention, a subscription at 13% monthly churn. He tightens the avatar, adds qualification, engineers an early win and communicates the work done.\n- **Offer mismatch with cold traffic (about 4 cases).** High-ticket offers pushed at strangers, commodity retainers. He lowers the first step (audit, front-end offer, pay per show) and ascends later.\n- **Price in the churn zone (2–3 cases).** Mid-priced SMB services churn fast; he says go cheap and automated with visible deliverables, or go up-market.\n- **Founder or partner as the sales problem (about 4 cases).** A founder who doesn't know sales, a partner who doesn't sell, an unmotivated hunter stuck farming. He puts the owner back into selling or forces the partner conversation.\n- **Sellability (about 5 cases).** Consulting and talent businesses are hard to sell; he points to recurring revenue, multiple channels, no founder dependency, or rolling up the clients.\n- **Supply before demand (2–3 cases).** Paused sales or a solo operator without margin; he fixes delivery leverage or price before adding leads.\n\n### Cases: E-commerce & Products (link: playbook/cases-products.html)\nTerm: Hotseat cases\nCard: Real hotseat situations from e-commerce brands, DTC labels, consumer goods and physical-product makers, with what Alex diagnosed and told the owner to do.\nKey rule: Most product owners have a pricing, focus or brand problem dressed up as a traffic problem; fix margin and the back end, then spend harder on the channel that already works.\nGist: Product owners usually arrive asking for more traffic or a new line. In most of these cases Alex finds the traffic is fine or under-tested, and the real gaps sit elsewhere: prices set by cost-plus habit, too many products or avatars competing for the same attention, no email or repeat-purchase back end, broken attribution, and a business that buys clicks profitably without ever becoming a brand anyone would pay a premium for or acquire.\n\nHis prescriptions follow a steady order. First subtract: cut the losing location, the side SKUs, the new venture that is really an escape. Then fix money per customer: test higher prices, add repeat-purchase offers and email flows, and make the most profitable segment easy to buy from. Only then scale acquisition, and when he does, he pushes owners to spend more and test more than feels comfortable on the channel that already returns well, treating profit as an experiment budget.\n\nTwo longer-term themes recur. Unbranded direct-response winners get copied and margins collapse as spend rises, so he steers owners with traction toward one product they believe in and a real brand, often built through creators and affiliates. And when an owner wants to bolt on something new (a SaaS, a character universe, an acquisition), he asks whether the existing engine is exhausted and who carries the risk; usually the answer is to keep the engine running and ...\nHow Alex would apply it:\n**First questions.**\n1. What are gross margin and net profit, and how were prices set?\n2. What share of customers buy again, and what do you send them after the first order?\n3. Which single channel and product make most of the profit, and can you prove it with attribution?\n4. What happens to returns if you double spend on the best channel?\n5. Is this a brand people would pay more for, or a product you sell because the ads work?\n6. What new thing are you about to start, and why now?\n\n**Default moves by situation.**\n- **Margin under ~10% or cost-plus pricing:** run price tests first, starting with the least price-sensitive line; close or cut anything losing money.\n- **No repeat purchases:** add in-box offers, email flows and a weekly nurture before buying more traffic.\n- **A channel returns well but growth stalls:** raise spend and widen targeting deliberately, with profit set aside as a learning budget; move into less-aware audiences with broader terms and bridge content.\n- **Attribution broken:** fix it before any other optimisation.\n- **Several SKUs or brands on media arbitrage:** keep the one the team believes in and build a brand around it, using creators or affiliates.\n- **Wanting a new line, a SaaS or an acquisition:** default answer is no. If the itch is real, keep the core engine running and fund the new thing from its profits as a marketing bet; refuse acquisitions with key-person risk or one-customer concentration.\n- **Cash squeeze:** sell to past buyers first (pre-orders, bundles), narrow to delivering the current product, and outsource or lease what is commoditised.\n- **A validated outbound or selling motion:** scale its volume and headcount before changing strategy.\nPatterns across cases:\n- **Subtract before adding (about 7 cases).** Losing location, extra SKUs, unfinished games, new categories, a SaaS escape: his first move is usually to cut or refuse something.\n- **The new venture is an escape (about 4 cases).** Software, womenswear, a character universe, an acquisition. He checks whether the core is exhausted; it rarely is.\n- **Prices set by habit (about 4 cases).** Cost-plus or below-market pricing; he prescribes value-based tests and direct price rises, noting that small increases move thin-margin profit a lot.\n- **Missing back end (about 4 cases).** No email flows, no repeat offer, past buyers never re-sold. E-commerce profit comes from the second and third purchase.\n- **Under-spending and under-testing the winning channel (about 4 cases).** Paid search at 10:1 that the owner is afraid to push, PPC never tried, outbound left at 100 emails a day.\n- **Media arbitrage without a brand (about 3 cases).** Profitable click-buying that caps out, gets copied and is hard to sell. Pick one product and build a brand, often through creators.\n- **Creators and partners as a channel (about 4 cases).** Influencer-affiliates, ambassador programs, trade partners who resell; give partners something they can earn on.\n- **Data before optimisation (about 2 cases).** Broken attribution or no predictive model means spend cannot be steered.\n- **Cash and inventory traps (about 3 cases).** Gross profit locked in stock or prototypes; he raises cash from past buyers and refocuses on shipping.\n\n### Cases: Education, Coaching & Software (link: playbook/cases-education-software.html)\nTerm: Hotseat cases\nCard: Real hotseat situations from coaches, course and community businesses, event and creator brands, and software companies, with what Alex diagnosed and told the owner to do.\nKey rule: Education is transactional, so growth equals acquisition; raise value per customer and fix activation before chasing a new avatar, product or platform.\nGist: Coaching, course and creator businesses tend to bring Alex the same three complaints: ads stopped scaling, customers churn, and the founder has run out of hours. Underneath, he usually finds that the owner wants to switch something big (platform, avatar, B2B, a new product) when the cheaper fix is inside the current funnel. Creative volume is too low, the weakest funnel step has never been tested, value per customer is too low to absorb rising acquisition costs, and the back end is sold too late or not at all. Many founders also over-deliver: Slack access, long unstructured calls and group sessions nobody attends, which caps capacity without raising retention.\n\nHis prescriptions are consistent. Keep the channel that works and feed it far more creative. Raise lifetime value first, through upsells, renewal pitches timed to milestones and events, annual prepay, performance components or a diagnostic sale of the outcome. Fix churn at the start: qualify for the customers who activate, onboard one-on-one, and deliver an early win. On price he moves both ways, but he warns hard against cutting price to fix churn, citing his own costly mistakes. When a founder is the bottleneck, he adds structure (a scripted webinar, a self-serve checkout, clipped content) before adding people.\n\nSoftware and sellability form a separate thread. For software he treats retention as the whole game: find ...\nHow Alex would apply it:\n**First questions.**\n1. What does a customer pay you over their lifetime, what does one cost to acquire, and how long until you are paid back?\n2. Which funnel step sits furthest below benchmark?\n3. How many ad creatives are live, and when did you last add new ones?\n4. Which customers activate and stay, and what do they have in common?\n5. What do customers buy after the first offer, and when do you pitch it?\n6. How many hours does the founder spend on delivery and DMs, and which of those do customers actually value?\n7. What new avatar, product or platform are you considering, and is the current one exhausted?\n\n**Default moves by situation.**\n- **Ads stopped scaling:** check creative volume and pixel setup first, then raise LTV with an upsell or back end; leave platform and model alone until those fail.\n- **LTV:CAC thin:** add a performance or renewal component, sell the goal over its timeframe, push annual prepay; only then spend more.\n- **High churn:** segment churners from stayers, qualify the front door to the stayers' profile, add one-on-one onboarding and an early win, and pitch renewals at milestones and events.\n- **Price questions:** treat very high close rates as a sign of underpricing; when a price jump stalls sales, shift part of the price to the back end; never cut price to fix churn.\n- **Founder at capacity:** strip over-delivery, structure the sale (scripted webinar, self-serve checkout), clip content for reach, then hire.\n- **Itch for something new:** add a channel inside the current business; refuse a new avatar or product until the core is clearly exhausted.\n- **Wanting an exit:** remove founder dependency and single-channel risk, or build around the sellable assets your students or audience create.\n- **Software:** prove retention in one cohort first, sell to whoever really decides, and commit to one path before spending on acquisition.\nPatterns across cases:\n- **Keep the working channel and feed it (about 10 cases).** Owners want a new platform, avatar or B2B line; he says more creative, more spend, more volume on what already returns, and adds a channel before he adds an avatar.\n- **The new thing is an escape (about 6 cases).** B2B, apparel and supplements, a business school, a new niche, SaaS: the plateau comes from a skill gap in the core, and a new line will not close it.\n- **Raise value per customer before scaling ads (about 9 cases).** Upsells, ~$1,500 one-time offers, performance back ends, diagnostic sales of the goal, renewal pitches, annual prepay and memberships for graduates.\n- **Churn is fixed at the start (about 6 cases).** Qualify for the avatar that activates, onboard one-on-one, book the next session, deliver an early win; he would rather lose sales than keep poor-fit buyers.\n- **Never cut price to fix churn (about 3 cases).** His own price cuts lost millions with churn unchanged; he raises or restructures price instead, splitting upfront and back-end when velocity suffers.\n- **Sell at events and milestones (about 4 cases).** Events and challenges pay off only when renewals and upgrades are pitched in the room.\n- **Over-delivery caps capacity (about 3 cases).** Chat access, long unstructured calls and unused group sessions; he cuts them and adds structure and self-serve checkout.\n- **Fix the weakest funnel step (about 3 cases).** Opt-in rate, pixel placement, cold traffic sent straight to checkout.\n- **Education is hard to sell (about 4 cases).** Founder dependency and single channels cap exit value; he points to the sellable assets students or audiences create.\n- **Software lives on retention and fit (about 4 cases).** Find the cohort that stays, sell to the real decision maker, commit to one path, and choose a growing market.\n";
